INSERT INTO sat_questions (question_id, external_id, vault_id, disclosed_item_id, source, program, test_type, test_type_desc, domain, primary_class_cd, skill_cd, skill_desc, difficulty, score_band_range_cd, question_type, stimulus_html, stem_html, answer_options, correct_answer, answer_keys, rationale_html, correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale, image_count, parent_template_name, parent_template_id, template_cluster_id, template_cluster_name, origin, position, answer_style, section, raw_payload, created_date, updated_date) VALUES
    ($SATQ$b507f90f$SATQ$, $SATQ$8957bc52-5dbe-4797-8706-718ddf96e23e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Latro, the main character of Gene Wolfe&rsquo;s novel <em>Soldier of the Mist</em>, is a man in ancient Greece. He completely loses his memory whenever he sleeps. So, before sleeping, Latro records the important experiences of each day on a scroll, carefully choosing which ones to <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b7d00104-2467-400b-9ff0-b13758f5b2b5","content":"<p>suspect</p>"},{"id":"f97ece12-28ec-43fa-88d5-578b5bf57c92","content":"<p>preserve</p>"},{"id":"8e38f352-48f0-40e7-9110-5f30f61f0894","content":"<p>suggest</p>"},{"id":"d0a05eda-2b82-44a6-91c5-e10c9877621f","content":"<p>imagine</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["f97ece12-28ec-43fa-88d5-578b5bf57c92"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text about Latro, the main character in Gene Wolfe&rsquo;s novel <em>Soldier of the Mist</em>. In this context, "preserve" means save or keep from destruction or loss. The text describes Latro as a character who loses his memory whenever he sleeps. The text goes on to explain that before going to sleep, Latro records his most important memories to prevent him from forgetting, or losing, them. The context therefore supports the idea that Latro records his most important experiences in an effort to save, or preserve, his memory of them from loss.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "suspect" would mean mistrust, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in this context. The text emphasizes that in writing down his experiences, Latro carefully chooses those which are most important to him and thus worth remembering later, not that he is mistrustful or has reason to doubt the experiences he records. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, "suggest" would mean propose for consideration. The text emphasizes the fact that Latro actually records those experiences that he has decided are most important to him, not that he merely proposes which experiences to consider for inclusion in his scroll. Choice D is incorrect because in this context, "imagine" would either mean form a mental image of or fabricate, neither of which would logically complete the text. Although it&rsquo;s possible that reviewing records from previous days might enable Latro to reconstruct mental images of what has happened to him in the past, the text emphasizes the fact that Latro carefully selects and writes down his experiences so he will remember them in the future, not so that he can form mental pictures of them as he writes them down. In a similar vein, the text emphasizes that Latro is recording experiences that have actually happened to him, not that he&rsquo;s fabricating the experiences he records.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"b507f90f","external_id":"8957bc52-5dbe-4797-8706-718ddf96e23e","vaultid":null,"uId":"fc40e866-95d4-4b36-aac2-82837af67db5","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Latro, the main character of Gene Wolfe&rsquo;s novel <em>Soldier of the Mist</em>, is a man in ancient Greece. He completely loses his memory whenever he sleeps. So, before sleeping, Latro records the important experiences of each day on a scroll, carefully choosing which ones to <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b7d00104-2467-400b-9ff0-b13758f5b2b5","content":"<p>suspect</p>"},{"id":"f97ece12-28ec-43fa-88d5-578b5bf57c92","content":"<p>preserve</p>"},{"id":"8e38f352-48f0-40e7-9110-5f30f61f0894","content":"<p>suggest</p>"},{"id":"d0a05eda-2b82-44a6-91c5-e10c9877621f","content":"<p>imagine</p>"}],"keys":["f97ece12-28ec-43fa-88d5-578b5bf57c92"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text about Latro, the main character in Gene Wolfe&rsquo;s novel <em>Soldier of the Mist</em>. In this context, \"preserve\" means save or keep from destruction or loss. The text describes Latro as a character who loses his memory whenever he sleeps. The text goes on to explain that before going to sleep, Latro records his most important memories to prevent him from forgetting, or losing, them. The context therefore supports the idea that Latro records his most important experiences in an effort to save, or preserve, his memory of them from loss.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"suspect\" would mean mistrust, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in this context. The text emphasizes that in writing down his experiences, Latro carefully chooses those which are most important to him and thus worth remembering later, not that he is mistrustful or has reason to doubt the experiences he records. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, \"suggest\" would mean propose for consideration. The text emphasizes the fact that Latro actually records those experiences that he has decided are most important to him, not that he merely proposes which experiences to consider for inclusion in his scroll. Choice D is incorrect because in this context, \"imagine\" would either mean form a mental image of or fabricate, neither of which would logically complete the text. Although it&rsquo;s possible that reviewing records from previous days might enable Latro to reconstruct mental images of what has happened to him in the past, the text emphasizes the fact that Latro carefully selects and writes down his experiences so he will remember them in the future, not so that he can form mental pictures of them as he writes them down. In a similar vein, the text emphasizes that Latro is recording experiences that have actually happened to him, not that he&rsquo;s fabricating the experiences he records.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323041,"updateDate":1730147323041}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323041, 1730147323041),
    ($SATQ$b5898291$SATQ$, $SATQ$0db84683-3874-4dec-9b2a-b95867879bc6$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The equipment from the Apollo Moon landings (1969&ndash;1972), such as radiation detectors and temperature probes, remains there to this day, but the data from these missions were mostly inaccessible until a recent data-transfer project made them <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>. This project has allowed researcher Seiichi Nagihara to make use of the information in investigating temperature changes on the Moon.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"3d390983-48ce-4364-bbda-7b3dd5bb6804","content":"<p>predictable</p>"},{"id":"8b51762b-fa1e-445e-b43c-c4215b9c97e1","content":"<p>complicated</p>"},{"id":"4cce14d3-ef14-42ae-98ae-21cce0188a33","content":"<p>representative</p>"},{"id":"6b409cb2-e2fa-4391-af95-e8cad28b8005","content":"<p>available</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6b409cb2-e2fa-4391-af95-e8cad28b8005"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of how a data-transfer project affected data from equipment from the Apollo Moon landings. In this context, "available" means accessible or able to be used. The text explains that the Apollo equipment is still on the Moon, but the data it collected "were mostly inaccessible" until a data-transfer project was undertaken. The text then states that this project allowed a researcher to use the information that has been collected. This context makes it clear that the project made previously inaccessible data available, or accessible.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because saying that a project made data from the Apollo equipment on the Moon "predictable" would mean that the data could be known or declared in advance, and it&rsquo;s not clear how that would be achieved, particularly with data that had been "mostly inaccessible" before the project. Rather than suggesting that the data-transfer project made the data predictable, the text conveys that it made the data available to be used as sources of information. Choice B is incorrect because the sentence indicates that data from the Apollo equipment on the Moon were "mostly inaccessible" until a recent data-transfer project did something to them, after which a researcher was able to make use of the data. This context conveys that the project made the data accessible and usable, not that it made them "complicated," or complex or difficult to understand. Choice C is incorrect. In this context, "representative" would mean either standing as a symbol of something or serving as a typical example from a group, and the text gives no indication that data retrieved from the Apollo equipment on the Moon was merely an example or symbolic. Rather, the text focuses on the availability and usefulness of the data, indicating that a data-transfer project enabled a researcher to use data that had previously been "mostly inaccessible."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"b5898291","external_id":"0db84683-3874-4dec-9b2a-b95867879bc6","vaultid":null,"uId":"2f41632b-cf84-4dac-bb92-147849e80dfb","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The equipment from the Apollo Moon landings (1969&ndash;1972), such as radiation detectors and temperature probes, remains there to this day, but the data from these missions were mostly inaccessible until a recent data-transfer project made them <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>. This project has allowed researcher Seiichi Nagihara to make use of the information in investigating temperature changes on the Moon.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"3d390983-48ce-4364-bbda-7b3dd5bb6804","content":"<p>predictable</p>"},{"id":"8b51762b-fa1e-445e-b43c-c4215b9c97e1","content":"<p>complicated</p>"},{"id":"4cce14d3-ef14-42ae-98ae-21cce0188a33","content":"<p>representative</p>"},{"id":"6b409cb2-e2fa-4391-af95-e8cad28b8005","content":"<p>available</p>"}],"keys":["6b409cb2-e2fa-4391-af95-e8cad28b8005"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of how a data-transfer project affected data from equipment from the Apollo Moon landings. In this context, \"available\" means accessible or able to be used. The text explains that the Apollo equipment is still on the Moon, but the data it collected \"were mostly inaccessible\" until a data-transfer project was undertaken. The text then states that this project allowed a researcher to use the information that has been collected. This context makes it clear that the project made previously inaccessible data available, or accessible.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because saying that a project made data from the Apollo equipment on the Moon \"predictable\" would mean that the data could be known or declared in advance, and it&rsquo;s not clear how that would be achieved, particularly with data that had been \"mostly inaccessible\" before the project. Rather than suggesting that the data-transfer project made the data predictable, the text conveys that it made the data available to be used as sources of information. Choice B is incorrect because the sentence indicates that data from the Apollo equipment on the Moon were \"mostly inaccessible\" until a recent data-transfer project did something to them, after which a researcher was able to make use of the data. This context conveys that the project made the data accessible and usable, not that it made them \"complicated,\" or complex or difficult to understand. Choice C is incorrect. In this context, \"representative\" would mean either standing as a symbol of something or serving as a typical example from a group, and the text gives no indication that data retrieved from the Apollo equipment on the Moon was merely an example or symbolic. Rather, the text focuses on the availability and usefulness of the data, indicating that a data-transfer project enabled a researcher to use data that had previously been \"mostly inaccessible.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061175,"updateDate":1755117061175}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061175, 1755117061175),
    ($SATQ$b622fb78$SATQ$, $SATQ$4ef63eaf-90f8-4e95-9136-6b1800994292$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is from the 1913 story &ldquo;The King&rsquo;s Coin&rdquo; by Emily Pauline Johnson, a Kanienkahagen (Mohawk) writer also known as Tekahionwake. Fox-Foot, a young Ojibwe man, is guiding a group of fur traders who are traveling by canoe and suspects that they are being followed.&nbsp;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>At supper time, Fox-Foot would allow no fire to be built, no landing to be made, no <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>trace</u></span> of their passing to be left. They ate canned meat and marmalade, drank again of the stream and pushed on, until just at dusk they reached the edge of a long, still lake, with shores of granite and dense fir forest.</p>
</blockquote>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;trace&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"77df2fa1-6a47-497c-ad11-03080ef53888","content":"<p>Evidence</p>"},{"id":"2f7fdbdf-942f-495b-b3f0-9a1f258e8d50","content":"<p>Blemish</p>"},{"id":"964ba3c1-4375-4bad-8b14-2286f91463b8","content":"<p>Amount</p>"},{"id":"201fb143-2777-4570-81fa-a3d2c915ffeb","content":"<p>Sketch</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["77df2fa1-6a47-497c-ad11-03080ef53888"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because the text indicates that Fox-Foot doesn&rsquo;t let the group build a fire or create a canoe landing when it&rsquo;s time for supper. This context suggests that he doesn&rsquo;t want anyone who might be following the group to see any sign of them or their activities. In other words, Fox-Foot doesn&rsquo;t want there to be any trace, or evidence, of the group&rsquo;s movements ("their passing") through the area.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text conveys that Fox-Foot doesn&rsquo;t want the group to be detected, not that he doesn&rsquo;t want their presence to create a blemish, or a spoiling flaw, in the area; human activity could disturb a natural environment, but the context emphasizes that Fox-Foot is instead focused on avoiding giving any sign of the group&rsquo;s movements through a place ("their passing") to anyone who might be following them. Choice C is incorrect because the text focuses on Fox-Foot&rsquo;s desire to avoid detection by those who might be following the group. This context conveys that Fox-Foot doesn&rsquo;t want to create any signs or evidence of the group moving through a place ("their passing"), not that he doesn&rsquo;t want to leave behind some quantity of their presence; indeed, it isn&rsquo;t clear what an amount of a group&rsquo;s movement would be. Choice D is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that the group has a sketch, or rough drawing, of their movements through that area ("their passing") that might be left behind. Rather, the context emphasizes that Fox-Foot is focused on ensuring that the group doesn&rsquo;t give any kind of
indication of their presence, as he wants to avoid detection by anyone who might be following the group.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"b622fb78","external_id":"4ef63eaf-90f8-4e95-9136-6b1800994292","vaultid":null,"uId":"c86cc867-bccf-4f10-b536-11c3d699177f","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is from the 1913 story &ldquo;The King&rsquo;s Coin&rdquo; by Emily Pauline Johnson, a Kanienkahagen (Mohawk) writer also known as Tekahionwake. Fox-Foot, a young Ojibwe man, is guiding a group of fur traders who are traveling by canoe and suspects that they are being followed.&nbsp;</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>At supper time, Fox-Foot would allow no fire to be built, no landing to be made, no <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>trace</u></span> of their passing to be left. They ate canned meat and marmalade, drank again of the stream and pushed on, until just at dusk they reached the edge of a long, still lake, with shores of granite and dense fir forest.</p>\n</blockquote>","stem":"<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;trace&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"77df2fa1-6a47-497c-ad11-03080ef53888","content":"<p>Evidence</p>"},{"id":"2f7fdbdf-942f-495b-b3f0-9a1f258e8d50","content":"<p>Blemish</p>"},{"id":"964ba3c1-4375-4bad-8b14-2286f91463b8","content":"<p>Amount</p>"},{"id":"201fb143-2777-4570-81fa-a3d2c915ffeb","content":"<p>Sketch</p>"}],"keys":["77df2fa1-6a47-497c-ad11-03080ef53888"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because the text indicates that Fox-Foot doesn&rsquo;t let the group build a fire or create a canoe landing when it&rsquo;s time for supper. This context suggests that he doesn&rsquo;t want anyone who might be following the group to see any sign of them or their activities. In other words, Fox-Foot doesn&rsquo;t want there to be any trace, or evidence, of the group&rsquo;s movements (\"their passing\") through the area.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text conveys that Fox-Foot doesn&rsquo;t want the group to be detected, not that he doesn&rsquo;t want their presence to create a blemish, or a spoiling flaw, in the area; human activity could disturb a natural environment, but the context emphasizes that Fox-Foot is instead focused on avoiding giving any sign of the group&rsquo;s movements through a place (\"their passing\") to anyone who might be following them. Choice C is incorrect because the text focuses on Fox-Foot&rsquo;s desire to avoid detection by those who might be following the group. This context conveys that Fox-Foot doesn&rsquo;t want to create any signs or evidence of the group moving through a place (\"their passing\"), not that he doesn&rsquo;t want to leave behind some quantity of their presence; indeed, it isn&rsquo;t clear what an amount of a group&rsquo;s movement would be. Choice D is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that the group has a sketch, or rough drawing, of their movements through that area (\"their passing\") that might be left behind. Rather, the context emphasizes that Fox-Foot is focused on ensuring that the group doesn&rsquo;t give any kind of\nindication of their presence, as he wants to avoid detection by anyone who might be following the group.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323032,"updateDate":1730147323032}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323032, 1730147323032),
    ($SATQ$b92c13fa$SATQ$, $SATQ$56a4297d-ab15-4d3c-b23e-95dd4b8f5317$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$4077c619-9487-4d99-937c-0e20a5c91bc0$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>According to statistician Nassim Nicholas Taleb, the best way to predict the amount of time a nonperishable entity (such as a building or a technology) will continue to exist is to examine how long it has survived so far. In this view, an item&rsquo;s age is the strongest <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> how much longer it will last.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"cccc6132-4462-4db4-b00b-597213212a00","content":"<p>uncertainty about</p>"},{"id":"c6768ee5-e080-44b8-b40c-502aa83353f5","content":"<p>indicator of</p>"},{"id":"e5fccf96-601f-4d96-a8c9-30d8bdcfefca","content":"<p>motivation for</p>"},{"id":"ad87b431-9fa6-4e80-86c1-85815355ca70","content":"<p>criticism of</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c6768ee5-e080-44b8-b40c-502aa83353f5"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. &ldquo;Indicator&rdquo; means &ldquo;something that shows or suggests,&rdquo; which matches Taleb&rsquo;s argument that an item&rsquo;s age can suggest how much longer it will last. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The passage tells us that examining an item&rsquo;s age is the best way to predict how much longer it will last. Therefore, according to Taleb&rsquo;s theory, an item&rsquo;s age should add more certainty about how much longer it will last. Choice C is incorrect. A &ldquo;motivation&rdquo; is &ldquo;a reason for doing.&rdquo; Items don&rsquo;t have feelings and motivations, so it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that their age is a motivation for how much longer they will last. Choice D is incorrect. &ldquo;Criticism&rdquo; can mean &ldquo;describing faults or problems&rdquo; or &ldquo;an analysis of an artistic work.&rdquo; Neither of these definitions makes sense here. An item&rsquo;s age can&rsquo;t criticize how much longer it will last. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-WIC-E009$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$27796c78-1b60-49bc-b14d-e6ccbda3bbba$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"b92c13fa","external_id":"56a4297d-ab15-4d3c-b23e-95dd4b8f5317","vaultid":"4077c619-9487-4d99-937c-0e20a5c91bc0","uId":"5ea0c036-ec11-48f6-bb0d-a06fed31ce44","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>According to statistician Nassim Nicholas Taleb, the best way to predict the amount of time a nonperishable entity (such as a building or a technology) will continue to exist is to examine how long it has survived so far. In this view, an item&rsquo;s age is the strongest <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> how much longer it will last.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"cccc6132-4462-4db4-b00b-597213212a00","content":"<p>uncertainty about</p>"},{"id":"c6768ee5-e080-44b8-b40c-502aa83353f5","content":"<p>indicator of</p>"},{"id":"e5fccf96-601f-4d96-a8c9-30d8bdcfefca","content":"<p>motivation for</p>"},{"id":"ad87b431-9fa6-4e80-86c1-85815355ca70","content":"<p>criticism of</p>"}],"keys":["c6768ee5-e080-44b8-b40c-502aa83353f5"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. &ldquo;Indicator&rdquo; means &ldquo;something that shows or suggests,&rdquo; which matches Taleb&rsquo;s argument that an item&rsquo;s age can suggest how much longer it will last. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The passage tells us that examining an item&rsquo;s age is the best way to predict how much longer it will last. Therefore, according to Taleb&rsquo;s theory, an item&rsquo;s age should add more certainty about how much longer it will last. Choice C is incorrect. A &ldquo;motivation&rdquo; is &ldquo;a reason for doing.&rdquo; Items don&rsquo;t have feelings and motivations, so it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that their age is a motivation for how much longer they will last. Choice D is incorrect. &ldquo;Criticism&rdquo; can mean &ldquo;describing faults or problems&rdquo; or &ldquo;an analysis of an artistic work.&rdquo; Neither of these definitions makes sense here. An item&rsquo;s age can&rsquo;t criticize how much longer it will last. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-WIC-E009","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"27796c78-1b60-49bc-b14d-e6ccbda3bbba","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959377,"updateDate":1691007959377}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959377, 1691007959377),
    ($SATQ$b93428a6$SATQ$, $SATQ$1c75f82a-dbd7-4499-80a8-42da5e0d22a9$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Animal researcher Amalia P.M. Bastos led a 2021 study about a wild kea parrot that used small stones as tools to preen its feathers. Skeptical colleagues had initially suggested to Bastos that the kea&rsquo;s interactions with the stones might simply be <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>, but Bastos and her team showed that the kea was using the stones deliberately.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"79c0ecd6-c55a-4403-b721-59b74e131c83","content":"<p>intriguing</p>"},{"id":"4fc604b5-51c3-415a-a1ff-99e1cdd610da","content":"<p>obvious</p>"},{"id":"cfc3b3ca-f4c4-4030-bc04-932db9974f9c","content":"<p>accidental</p>"},{"id":"dca3ac11-ac1c-48b8-8a46-c0635404892c","content":"<p>observable</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["cfc3b3ca-f4c4-4030-bc04-932db9974f9c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Bastos&rsquo;s study of a wild kea parrot. In this context, "accidental" means unplanned or unintentional. The text first describes Bastos&rsquo;s study, which concerns a kea that is observed using small stones to preen its feathers. The text then mentions colleagues who are skeptical (that is, they have doubt) about Bastos&rsquo;s findings, and finally describes how Bastos and her team responded to the skepticism of those colleagues. Given that the colleagues mentioned in the text expressed skepticism regarding Bastos&rsquo;s findings, the best answer choice must be one that completes the text in a manner such that the skeptics&rsquo; opinion regarding the kea&rsquo;s use of stones disagrees with that held by Bastos and her team. Since Bastos and her team showed that the kea&rsquo;s use of stones was deliberate (that is, intentional), the skeptics&rsquo; opinion in this context must be that the kea&rsquo;s use of stones was unintentional, or accidental.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the best answer choice is one that portrays skepticism, or doubt, of Bastos&rsquo;s claim that the kea&rsquo;s usage of stones was deliberate, or intentional. If the skeptics found the kea&rsquo;s usage of stones "intriguing," or fascinating, this would not be at odds with the position of Bastos and her team; in fact, it is reasonable to believe that someone who agreed that the kea&rsquo;s stone usage was deliberate would also find it intriguing. Choice B is incorrect because if the skeptics believed that the kea&rsquo;s usage of small stones was "obvious," or evident, this would not be contrary to the observation of Bastos and her team that the kea&rsquo;s usage of stones was deliberate: in fact, these opinions would be consistent with each other. Choice D is incorrect because if the skeptics believed that the kea&rsquo;s usage of small stones was "observable," or visible, this would not conflict with the claim of Bastos and her team that the kea&rsquo;s usage of stones was deliberate: instead, these positions would agree.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"b93428a6","external_id":"1c75f82a-dbd7-4499-80a8-42da5e0d22a9","vaultid":null,"uId":"e4477607-9816-43f5-906c-7d58ba1e5a0b","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Animal researcher Amalia P.M. Bastos led a 2021 study about a wild kea parrot that used small stones as tools to preen its feathers. Skeptical colleagues had initially suggested to Bastos that the kea&rsquo;s interactions with the stones might simply be <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>, but Bastos and her team showed that the kea was using the stones deliberately.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"79c0ecd6-c55a-4403-b721-59b74e131c83","content":"<p>intriguing</p>"},{"id":"4fc604b5-51c3-415a-a1ff-99e1cdd610da","content":"<p>obvious</p>"},{"id":"cfc3b3ca-f4c4-4030-bc04-932db9974f9c","content":"<p>accidental</p>"},{"id":"dca3ac11-ac1c-48b8-8a46-c0635404892c","content":"<p>observable</p>"}],"keys":["cfc3b3ca-f4c4-4030-bc04-932db9974f9c"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Bastos&rsquo;s study of a wild kea parrot. In this context, \"accidental\" means unplanned or unintentional. The text first describes Bastos&rsquo;s study, which concerns a kea that is observed using small stones to preen its feathers. The text then mentions colleagues who are skeptical (that is, they have doubt) about Bastos&rsquo;s findings, and finally describes how Bastos and her team responded to the skepticism of those colleagues. Given that the colleagues mentioned in the text expressed skepticism regarding Bastos&rsquo;s findings, the best answer choice must be one that completes the text in a manner such that the skeptics&rsquo; opinion regarding the kea&rsquo;s use of stones disagrees with that held by Bastos and her team. Since Bastos and her team showed that the kea&rsquo;s use of stones was deliberate (that is, intentional), the skeptics&rsquo; opinion in this context must be that the kea&rsquo;s use of stones was unintentional, or accidental.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the best answer choice is one that portrays skepticism, or doubt, of Bastos&rsquo;s claim that the kea&rsquo;s usage of stones was deliberate, or intentional. If the skeptics found the kea&rsquo;s usage of stones \"intriguing,\" or fascinating, this would not be at odds with the position of Bastos and her team; in fact, it is reasonable to believe that someone who agreed that the kea&rsquo;s stone usage was deliberate would also find it intriguing. Choice B is incorrect because if the skeptics believed that the kea&rsquo;s usage of small stones was \"obvious,\" or evident, this would not be contrary to the observation of Bastos and her team that the kea&rsquo;s usage of stones was deliberate: in fact, these opinions would be consistent with each other. Choice D is incorrect because if the skeptics believed that the kea&rsquo;s usage of small stones was \"observable,\" or visible, this would not conflict with the claim of Bastos and her team that the kea&rsquo;s usage of stones was deliberate: instead, these positions would agree.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323032,"updateDate":1730147323032}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323032, 1730147323032),
    ($SATQ$b940952c$SATQ$, $SATQ$275ad6c6-c431-4660-a1ce-0de13c039334$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>On the basis of extensive calculations and models, astronomers in the 1990s predicted that the collision of two neutron stars or a neutron star and a black hole could release a massive burst of gamma rays in an event called a kilonova. This <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> was confirmed with observations in 2017.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"6fd38443-000c-4e4e-84fc-6bf4866b3711","content":"<p>theory</p>"},{"id":"13cb4948-bd34-4c0f-800a-8304b3ae1e34","content":"<p>evidence</p>"},{"id":"1f42485a-6c3a-4d35-9281-bad16638673f","content":"<p>constant</p>"},{"id":"5bd163d4-feeb-4241-b878-d3b3dac376a3","content":"<p>experiment</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6fd38443-000c-4e4e-84fc-6bf4866b3711"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of a prediction about a kilonova. In this context, a "theory" is an explanation that is considered scientifically acceptable. The text states that astronomers predicted in the 1990s that a collision between a black hole and a neutron star or between two neutron stars could release a massive gamma ray burst called a kilonova, explaining that they determined this possibility based on their extensive work with existing data and simulations ("calculations and models"). In other words, the prediction was a theory&mdash;a well-supported explanation&mdash;that, as the text indicates, was later confirmed with observations in 2017.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text indicates that it is the prediction made by astronomers in the 1990s that was confirmed in 2017, and a prediction of an event isn&rsquo;t "evidence," or proof, of that event&rsquo;s existence, even when the prediction is based on extensive study. Further, there would be no need for later confirmation of something that was already recognized as evidence. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, a "constant" is a situation or factor that doesn&rsquo;t change. The text indicates that it is the prediction made by astronomers in the 1990s that was confirmed in 2017, and there is no reason to describe the prediction as a constant because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the prediction was completely unchanged over time&mdash;it addresses only the making of the prediction and its later confirmation. Choice D is incorrect because the text indicates that it is the prediction made by astronomers in the 1990s that was confirmed in 2017; although a prediction might be informed by an "experiment," or a controlled test, a prediction is an idea rather than a test.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"b940952c","external_id":"275ad6c6-c431-4660-a1ce-0de13c039334","vaultid":null,"uId":"95469d99-282f-43d8-976c-0efc438fa4f9","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>On the basis of extensive calculations and models, astronomers in the 1990s predicted that the collision of two neutron stars or a neutron star and a black hole could release a massive burst of gamma rays in an event called a kilonova. This <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> was confirmed with observations in 2017.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"6fd38443-000c-4e4e-84fc-6bf4866b3711","content":"<p>theory</p>"},{"id":"13cb4948-bd34-4c0f-800a-8304b3ae1e34","content":"<p>evidence</p>"},{"id":"1f42485a-6c3a-4d35-9281-bad16638673f","content":"<p>constant</p>"},{"id":"5bd163d4-feeb-4241-b878-d3b3dac376a3","content":"<p>experiment</p>"}],"keys":["6fd38443-000c-4e4e-84fc-6bf4866b3711"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of a prediction about a kilonova. In this context, a \"theory\" is an explanation that is considered scientifically acceptable. The text states that astronomers predicted in the 1990s that a collision between a black hole and a neutron star or between two neutron stars could release a massive gamma ray burst called a kilonova, explaining that they determined this possibility based on their extensive work with existing data and simulations (\"calculations and models\"). In other words, the prediction was a theory&mdash;a well-supported explanation&mdash;that, as the text indicates, was later confirmed with observations in 2017.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text indicates that it is the prediction made by astronomers in the 1990s that was confirmed in 2017, and a prediction of an event isn&rsquo;t \"evidence,\" or proof, of that event&rsquo;s existence, even when the prediction is based on extensive study. Further, there would be no need for later confirmation of something that was already recognized as evidence. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, a \"constant\" is a situation or factor that doesn&rsquo;t change. The text indicates that it is the prediction made by astronomers in the 1990s that was confirmed in 2017, and there is no reason to describe the prediction as a constant because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the prediction was completely unchanged over time&mdash;it addresses only the making of the prediction and its later confirmation. Choice D is incorrect because the text indicates that it is the prediction made by astronomers in the 1990s that was confirmed in 2017; although a prediction might be informed by an \"experiment,\" or a controlled test, a prediction is an idea rather than a test.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669204,"updateDate":1743430669204}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669204, 1743430669204),
    ($SATQ$ba974387$SATQ$, $SATQ$21100c10-4a3a-4a15-93d0-9c8e29687269$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from&nbsp;Mary Seacole&rsquo;s <span data-ssml-say-as="date">1857</span> autobiography <em>Wonderful Adventures of <span data-ssml-sub-alias="Mrs">Mrs.</span> Seacole in Many Lands</em>.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>That journey across the Isthmus [of Panama], insignificant in distance as it was, was by no means an easy one. It seemed as if nature had determined to throw every <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>conceivable</u></span> obstacle in the way of those who should seek to join the two great oceans of the world.</p>
</blockquote>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;conceivable&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"caa3e3d4-2ad8-4032-ad70-b7cb5934916b","content":"<p>Obvious</p>"},{"id":"5bf5dea0-284d-4e43-bed0-49a5478d850d","content":"<p>Imaginable</p>"},{"id":"4714be7d-7100-4e13-8b06-83a90c5b08ff","content":"<p>Straightforward</p>"},{"id":"3c0922f3-7ab4-40f2-ac86-467011357b54","content":"<p>Uninterrupted</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["5bf5dea0-284d-4e43-bed0-49a5478d850d"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because as used in the text, "conceivable" most nearly means capable of being thought of or imagined. The text describes a journey that, despite being "insignificant in distance," was "by no means an easy one" because of the variety of obstacles that "nature had determined to throw&hellip;in the way." To emphasize the difficulty of the journey, the author portrays nature as actively scheming to present travelers with every obstacle that can be thought of&mdash;i.e., every imaginable obstacle.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context, "conceivable" does not mean obvious, or evident. Instead, by referring to "every conceivable obstacle," the text suggests a wide variety of challenges by evoking all the potential difficulties nature could present, not just those that the travelers might reasonably expect or foresee. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, "conceivable" does not mean straightforward, or easy to negotiate. The text is not indicating that the obstacles were simple to respond to and overcome. Rather, the text suggests that a wide range of obstacles made the journey difficult. Choice D is incorrect because in this context, "conceivable" does not mean uninterrupted. Although the text suggests that the obstacles were frequent and numerous, it does not emphasize the obstacles&rsquo; continuity; instead, the text conveys their unexpected variety and range.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"ba974387","external_id":"21100c10-4a3a-4a15-93d0-9c8e29687269","vaultid":null,"uId":"722a8038-b0a5-4103-bf40-a19c404b29ef","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from&nbsp;Mary Seacole&rsquo;s <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1857</span> autobiography <em>Wonderful Adventures of <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"Mrs\">Mrs.</span> Seacole in Many Lands</em>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>That journey across the Isthmus [of Panama], insignificant in distance as it was, was by no means an easy one. It seemed as if nature had determined to throw every <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>conceivable</u></span> obstacle in the way of those who should seek to join the two great oceans of the world.</p>\n</blockquote>","stem":"<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;conceivable&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"caa3e3d4-2ad8-4032-ad70-b7cb5934916b","content":"<p>Obvious</p>"},{"id":"5bf5dea0-284d-4e43-bed0-49a5478d850d","content":"<p>Imaginable</p>"},{"id":"4714be7d-7100-4e13-8b06-83a90c5b08ff","content":"<p>Straightforward</p>"},{"id":"3c0922f3-7ab4-40f2-ac86-467011357b54","content":"<p>Uninterrupted</p>"}],"keys":["5bf5dea0-284d-4e43-bed0-49a5478d850d"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because as used in the text, \"conceivable\" most nearly means capable of being thought of or imagined. The text describes a journey that, despite being \"insignificant in distance,\" was \"by no means an easy one\" because of the variety of obstacles that \"nature had determined to throw&hellip;in the way.\" To emphasize the difficulty of the journey, the author portrays nature as actively scheming to present travelers with every obstacle that can be thought of&mdash;i.e., every imaginable obstacle.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context, \"conceivable\" does not mean obvious, or evident. Instead, by referring to \"every conceivable obstacle,\" the text suggests a wide variety of challenges by evoking all the potential difficulties nature could present, not just those that the travelers might reasonably expect or foresee. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, \"conceivable\" does not mean straightforward, or easy to negotiate. The text is not indicating that the obstacles were simple to respond to and overcome. Rather, the text suggests that a wide range of obstacles made the journey difficult. Choice D is incorrect because in this context, \"conceivable\" does not mean uninterrupted. Although the text suggests that the obstacles were frequent and numerous, it does not emphasize the obstacles&rsquo; continuity; instead, the text conveys their unexpected variety and range.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061176,"updateDate":1755117061176}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061176, 1755117061176),
    ($SATQ$bbb77c84$SATQ$, $SATQ$74a079ad-8f88-4f78-b283-59cabdf3c8ac$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Containing over 160 billion base pairs of DNA resulting from polyploidy (whole genome duplication) and accumulation of noncoding DNA without significant deletion, the genome of <em>Tmesipteris oblanceolata</em> (New Caledonian fork fern) is the largest known among eukaryotes&mdash;and, given the high biological costs of extremely large genomes, is unlikely to be <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"fcc7052b-748a-47cd-bf45-f41d1f325fcb","content":"<p>protracted</p>"},{"id":"d11e833b-26db-4eff-878a-cc2a1cb4cf50","content":"<p>superseded</p>"},{"id":"a554f3dd-c42f-41d4-bf95-392036c48181","content":"<p>curtailed</p>"},{"id":"bf5d22bd-2fb9-441e-b578-68b421d0bc4f","content":"<p>obviated</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["d11e833b-26db-4eff-878a-cc2a1cb4cf50"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the fork fern&rsquo;s enormous genome. In this context, "superseded" means surpassed or exceeded by something else. The text establishes that this plant has "the largest known" genome among eukaryotes and mentions "the high biological costs of extremely large genomes," suggesting there are natural constraints that make it biologically disadvantageous to maintain such massive genetic material. This context supports the idea that it&rsquo;s unlikely another organism will evolve an even larger genome that would replace this plant&rsquo;s record&mdash;that is, its status as having the largest genome is unlikely to be superseded.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context, "protracted" would mean drawn out over time or space, and the text focuses on the record size of the New Caledonian fork fern&rsquo;s genome and the fact that extremely large genomes carry "high biological costs." This information suggests that the biological upper limit for genome size may have been reached and that the New Caledonian fork fern&rsquo;s genome is therefore unlikely to be surpassed, or superseded, not that it&rsquo;s unlikely to be protracted. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, "curtailed" would mean cut short or reduced, and the text focuses on the record size of the New Caledonian fork fern&rsquo;s genome and the fact that extremely large genomes carry "high biological costs." This information suggests that the biological upper limit for genome size may have been reached and that the New Caledonian fork fern&rsquo;s genome is therefore unlikely to be surpassed, or superseded, not that it&rsquo;s unlikely to be curtailed. Choice D is incorrect because in this context, "obviated" would mean made unnecessary or redundant, and the text focuses on the record size of the New Caledonian fork fern&rsquo;s genome and the fact that extremely large genomes carry "high biological costs." This information suggests that the biological upper limit for genome size may have been reached and that the New Caledonian fork fern&rsquo;s genome is therefore unlikely to be surpassed, or superseded, not that it&rsquo;s unlikely to be obviated.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"bbb77c84","external_id":"74a079ad-8f88-4f78-b283-59cabdf3c8ac","vaultid":null,"uId":"92ac6f35-ac5c-49e8-ad59-0eda77b15c0f","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Containing over 160 billion base pairs of DNA resulting from polyploidy (whole genome duplication) and accumulation of noncoding DNA without significant deletion, the genome of <em>Tmesipteris oblanceolata</em> (New Caledonian fork fern) is the largest known among eukaryotes&mdash;and, given the high biological costs of extremely large genomes, is unlikely to be <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"fcc7052b-748a-47cd-bf45-f41d1f325fcb","content":"<p>protracted</p>"},{"id":"d11e833b-26db-4eff-878a-cc2a1cb4cf50","content":"<p>superseded</p>"},{"id":"a554f3dd-c42f-41d4-bf95-392036c48181","content":"<p>curtailed</p>"},{"id":"bf5d22bd-2fb9-441e-b578-68b421d0bc4f","content":"<p>obviated</p>"}],"keys":["d11e833b-26db-4eff-878a-cc2a1cb4cf50"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the fork fern&rsquo;s enormous genome. In this context, \"superseded\" means surpassed or exceeded by something else. The text establishes that this plant has \"the largest known\" genome among eukaryotes and mentions \"the high biological costs of extremely large genomes,\" suggesting there are natural constraints that make it biologically disadvantageous to maintain such massive genetic material. This context supports the idea that it&rsquo;s unlikely another organism will evolve an even larger genome that would replace this plant&rsquo;s record&mdash;that is, its status as having the largest genome is unlikely to be superseded.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context, \"protracted\" would mean drawn out over time or space, and the text focuses on the record size of the New Caledonian fork fern&rsquo;s genome and the fact that extremely large genomes carry \"high biological costs.\" This information suggests that the biological upper limit for genome size may have been reached and that the New Caledonian fork fern&rsquo;s genome is therefore unlikely to be surpassed, or superseded, not that it&rsquo;s unlikely to be protracted. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, \"curtailed\" would mean cut short or reduced, and the text focuses on the record size of the New Caledonian fork fern&rsquo;s genome and the fact that extremely large genomes carry \"high biological costs.\" This information suggests that the biological upper limit for genome size may have been reached and that the New Caledonian fork fern&rsquo;s genome is therefore unlikely to be surpassed, or superseded, not that it&rsquo;s unlikely to be curtailed. Choice D is incorrect because in this context, \"obviated\" would mean made unnecessary or redundant, and the text focuses on the record size of the New Caledonian fork fern&rsquo;s genome and the fact that extremely large genomes carry \"high biological costs.\" This information suggests that the biological upper limit for genome size may have been reached and that the New Caledonian fork fern&rsquo;s genome is therefore unlikely to be surpassed, or superseded, not that it&rsquo;s unlikely to be obviated.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061178,"updateDate":1755117061178}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061178, 1755117061178),
    ($SATQ$bcc91b1e$SATQ$, $SATQ$8fed581f-1329-4cb7-8727-8c2037159d3d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$93281b45-91da-4708-bbf3-5ecd2361aa8b$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The spacecraft OSIRIS-REx briefly made contact with the asteroid 101955 Bennu in 2020. NASA scientist Daniella DellaGiustina reports that despite facing the unexpected obstacle of a surface mostly covered in boulders, OSIRIS-REx successfully <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> a sample of the surface, gathering pieces of it to bring back to Earth.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a1aaacac-468e-46b3-9053-e5a4a21aaf59","content":"<p>attached</p>"},{"id":"9d4cd81d-e9b2-4cc0-9409-5e0db664d9e2","content":"<p>collected</p>"},{"id":"a93aa444-8ff6-493c-9a97-3d8258a37bf3","content":"<p>followed</p>"},{"id":"59f750ba-87a9-4c16-af76-30d1fa04e0b1","content":"<p>replaced</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["9d4cd81d-e9b2-4cc0-9409-5e0db664d9e2"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the OSIRIS-REx spacecraft&rsquo;s contact with the asteroid 101955 Bennu. In this context, &ldquo;collected&rdquo; means acquired and took away. The text indicates that although the boulders on the asteroid&rsquo;s surface caused some unforeseen problems, OSIRIS-REx was able to gather a sample to return to Earth. This context suggests that OSIRIS-REx successfully collected a sample of 101955 Bennu. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context &ldquo;attached&rdquo; means connected or affixed. The text indicates that OSIRIS-REx gathered pieces of 101955 Bennu to bring to Earth; it doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the spacecraft attached anything to the asteroid. Choice C is incorrect because in this context &ldquo;followed&rdquo; means tracked or traveled behind and the text discusses OSIRIS-REx&rsquo;s brief encounter with 101955 Bennu during which the spacecraft gathered a sample to bring to Earth. The text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the spacecraft tracked the sample, and it&rsquo;s not clear what it would mean for the spacecraft to travel behind the sample it collected.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because in this context &ldquo;replaced&rdquo; means put back or returned. The text indicates that OSIRIS-REx gathered pieces of 101955 Bennu to bring to Earth but doesn&rsquo;t suggest that anything was returned to the asteroid. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-961-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$62ce2209-4e77-48b1-ac76-29ce9773c8c5$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"bcc91b1e","external_id":"8fed581f-1329-4cb7-8727-8c2037159d3d","vaultid":"93281b45-91da-4708-bbf3-5ecd2361aa8b","uId":"862fe76c-9ce4-4dd8-8d70-57283068b9fe","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The spacecraft OSIRIS-REx briefly made contact with the asteroid 101955 Bennu in 2020. NASA scientist Daniella DellaGiustina reports that despite facing the unexpected obstacle of a surface mostly covered in boulders, OSIRIS-REx successfully <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> a sample of the surface, gathering pieces of it to bring back to Earth.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a1aaacac-468e-46b3-9053-e5a4a21aaf59","content":"<p>attached</p>"},{"id":"9d4cd81d-e9b2-4cc0-9409-5e0db664d9e2","content":"<p>collected</p>"},{"id":"a93aa444-8ff6-493c-9a97-3d8258a37bf3","content":"<p>followed</p>"},{"id":"59f750ba-87a9-4c16-af76-30d1fa04e0b1","content":"<p>replaced</p>"}],"keys":["9d4cd81d-e9b2-4cc0-9409-5e0db664d9e2"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the OSIRIS-REx spacecraft&rsquo;s contact with the asteroid 101955 Bennu. In this context, &ldquo;collected&rdquo; means acquired and took away. The text indicates that although the boulders on the asteroid&rsquo;s surface caused some unforeseen problems, OSIRIS-REx was able to gather a sample to return to Earth. This context suggests that OSIRIS-REx successfully collected a sample of 101955 Bennu. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context &ldquo;attached&rdquo; means connected or affixed. The text indicates that OSIRIS-REx gathered pieces of 101955 Bennu to bring to Earth; it doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the spacecraft attached anything to the asteroid. Choice C is incorrect because in this context &ldquo;followed&rdquo; means tracked or traveled behind and the text discusses OSIRIS-REx&rsquo;s brief encounter with 101955 Bennu during which the spacecraft gathered a sample to bring to Earth. The text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the spacecraft tracked the sample, and it&rsquo;s not clear what it would mean for the spacecraft to travel behind the sample it collected.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because in this context &ldquo;replaced&rdquo; means put back or returned. The text indicates that OSIRIS-REx gathered pieces of 101955 Bennu to bring to Earth but doesn&rsquo;t suggest that anything was returned to the asteroid. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-961-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"62ce2209-4e77-48b1-ac76-29ce9773c8c5","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959357,"updateDate":1691007959357}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959357, 1691007959357),
    ($SATQ$bcd924a5$SATQ$, $SATQ$8ec1be26-3414-4bbe-a886-6cc14889c7e2$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The unique <em>subak</em> water management system used to irrigate the rice paddy fields of the Indonesian island of Bali has a rich cultural, philosophical, and historical significance dating back to the ninth century. The many elements of subak&mdash;terraces, canals, and water temples&mdash;are <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>: they are joined together into a single cohesive unit.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"128666b0-8dee-4ae3-b72c-0f5a64f33ab5","content":"<p>outmoded</p>"},{"id":"94144b23-7950-4563-8fe9-a743f68f674e","content":"<p>informal</p>"},{"id":"37bb628f-b140-4b4e-8604-f3a27eb18e19","content":"<p>interconnected</p>"},{"id":"d8d95528-e969-4302-9a14-ec8e05e5fede","content":"<p>optional</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["37bb628f-b140-4b4e-8604-f3a27eb18e19"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Bali&rsquo;s <em>subak </em>water management system. In this context, &ldquo;interconnected&rdquo; would mean joined or linked together. The text describes the various components of the<em> </em>subak system&mdash;&ldquo;terraces, canals, and water temples&rdquo;&mdash;and then states that they are &ldquo;joined together into a single cohesive unit.&rdquo; This context indicates that these elements are connected with one another in an integrated system.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;outmoded&rdquo; would mean outdated or no longer in use. While the text does indicate that the subak<em> </em>system has &ldquo;rich cultural, philosophical, and historical significance,&rdquo; the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the subak<em> </em>system is obsolete or not currently used. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;informal&rdquo; would suggest that the subak<em> </em>system lacks structure or organization. The text contradicts this by describing a complex system with specific components (&ldquo;terraces, canals, and water temples&rdquo;) that are &ldquo;joined together into a single cohesive unit,&rdquo; indicating formal organization rather than informality. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;optional&rdquo; would suggest that the various elements of the subak<em> </em>system are not necessary or required. The text indicates the opposite by stating that these elements are &ldquo;joined together into a single cohesive unit,&rdquo; suggesting that each component plays a necessary role in the functioning of the integrated system.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"bcd924a5","external_id":"8ec1be26-3414-4bbe-a886-6cc14889c7e2","vaultid":null,"uId":"a43fdfd6-6118-4e1b-ae3f-bc76b67200ae","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The unique <em>subak</em> water management system used to irrigate the rice paddy fields of the Indonesian island of Bali has a rich cultural, philosophical, and historical significance dating back to the ninth century. The many elements of subak&mdash;terraces, canals, and water temples&mdash;are <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>: they are joined together into a single cohesive unit.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"128666b0-8dee-4ae3-b72c-0f5a64f33ab5","content":"<p>outmoded</p>"},{"id":"94144b23-7950-4563-8fe9-a743f68f674e","content":"<p>informal</p>"},{"id":"37bb628f-b140-4b4e-8604-f3a27eb18e19","content":"<p>interconnected</p>"},{"id":"d8d95528-e969-4302-9a14-ec8e05e5fede","content":"<p>optional</p>"}],"keys":["37bb628f-b140-4b4e-8604-f3a27eb18e19"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Bali&rsquo;s <em>subak </em>water management system. In this context, &ldquo;interconnected&rdquo; would mean joined or linked together. The text describes the various components of the<em> </em>subak system&mdash;&ldquo;terraces, canals, and water temples&rdquo;&mdash;and then states that they are &ldquo;joined together into a single cohesive unit.&rdquo; This context indicates that these elements are connected with one another in an integrated system.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;outmoded&rdquo; would mean outdated or no longer in use. While the text does indicate that the subak<em> </em>system has &ldquo;rich cultural, philosophical, and historical significance,&rdquo; the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the subak<em> </em>system is obsolete or not currently used. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;informal&rdquo; would suggest that the subak<em> </em>system lacks structure or organization. The text contradicts this by describing a complex system with specific components (&ldquo;terraces, canals, and water temples&rdquo;) that are &ldquo;joined together into a single cohesive unit,&rdquo; indicating formal organization rather than informality. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;optional&rdquo; would suggest that the various elements of the subak<em> </em>system are not necessary or required. The text indicates the opposite by stating that these elements are &ldquo;joined together into a single cohesive unit,&rdquo; suggesting that each component plays a necessary role in the functioning of the integrated system.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771169,"updateDate":1776362771169}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771169, 1776362771169),
    ($SATQ$bce4d106$SATQ$, $SATQ$c8685aba-8be7-472a-b8de-550578033409$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Archaeologists have identified a plethora of beads fashioned from <em>Tritia gibbosula</em> shells in many Middle Stone Age (<span data-ssml-say-as="characters">MSA</span>) sites across western North Africa, including El Mnasra Cave, Morocco. In a <span data-ssml-say-as="date">2021</span> paper, El Mehdi Sehasseh <span data-ssml-sub-alias="et al">et al.</span> attribute these artifacts&rsquo; apparent <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> to the evolution and propagation of symbolic behavior (<span data-ssml-sub-alias="e g">e.g.</span>, use of personal ornaments) in humans and the widespread availability of <em><span data-ssml-sub-alias="T">T.</span> gibbosula</em> during the MSA.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"3336ba49-9b18-4087-a374-fda630bf0a6b","content":"<p>heterogeneity</p>"},{"id":"89409fee-2d5e-4348-b62e-f448d1f00419","content":"<p>ubiquity</p>"},{"id":"17338846-782d-42d2-8afd-ae6d1d7e89d8","content":"<p>expediency</p>"},{"id":"789b6f4b-0356-45f2-bdac-538c5764b66f","content":"<p>fecundity</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["89409fee-2d5e-4348-b62e-f448d1f00419"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of beads found in Middle Stone Age sites. In this context, &ldquo;ubiquity&rdquo; means widespread presence or commonness. The text states that archaeologists have encountered &ldquo;a plethora,&rdquo; or an abundance, of beads made from <em>Tritia gibbosula</em> shells &ldquo;in many Middle Stone Age (MSA) sites across western North Africa.&rdquo; In this context, the abundance of beads therefore suggests their ubiquity.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context, &ldquo;heterogeneity&rdquo; would mean variety or diversity among the beads. The text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the beads varied significantly from one another; rather, it focuses on their widespread presence across multiple sites. Moreover, the text mentions that all the beads were &ldquo;fashioned from <em>Tritia gibbosula</em> shells,&rdquo; suggesting consistency rather than heterogeneity. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the beads&rsquo; &ldquo;expediency,&rdquo; or convenience or practicality. The text doesn&rsquo;t support the idea of convenience (there&rsquo;s no indication, for example, that the beads were easy to use or make) but rather focuses on the beads&rsquo; abundant presence at MSA sites. Choice D is incorrect. While &ldquo;fecundity&rdquo; can suggest abundant production, it specifically refers to fertility, and the text doesn&rsquo;t connect the beads to this theme. Instead, the text attributes their widespread presence to the development of symbolic behavior in humans and the natural availability of <em>Tritia gibbosula</em> during the MSA.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"bce4d106","external_id":"c8685aba-8be7-472a-b8de-550578033409","vaultid":null,"uId":"90ab95a0-8794-46f4-979b-6cf641888008","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Archaeologists have identified a plethora of beads fashioned from <em>Tritia gibbosula</em> shells in many Middle Stone Age (<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">MSA</span>) sites across western North Africa, including El Mnasra Cave, Morocco. In a <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">2021</span> paper, El Mehdi Sehasseh <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"et al\">et al.</span> attribute these artifacts&rsquo; apparent <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> to the evolution and propagation of symbolic behavior (<span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"e g\">e.g.</span>, use of personal ornaments) in humans and the widespread availability of <em><span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"T\">T.</span> gibbosula</em> during the MSA.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"3336ba49-9b18-4087-a374-fda630bf0a6b","content":"<p>heterogeneity</p>"},{"id":"89409fee-2d5e-4348-b62e-f448d1f00419","content":"<p>ubiquity</p>"},{"id":"17338846-782d-42d2-8afd-ae6d1d7e89d8","content":"<p>expediency</p>"},{"id":"789b6f4b-0356-45f2-bdac-538c5764b66f","content":"<p>fecundity</p>"}],"keys":["89409fee-2d5e-4348-b62e-f448d1f00419"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of beads found in Middle Stone Age sites. In this context, &ldquo;ubiquity&rdquo; means widespread presence or commonness. The text states that archaeologists have encountered &ldquo;a plethora,&rdquo; or an abundance, of beads made from <em>Tritia gibbosula</em> shells &ldquo;in many Middle Stone Age (MSA) sites across western North Africa.&rdquo; In this context, the abundance of beads therefore suggests their ubiquity.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context, &ldquo;heterogeneity&rdquo; would mean variety or diversity among the beads. The text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the beads varied significantly from one another; rather, it focuses on their widespread presence across multiple sites. Moreover, the text mentions that all the beads were &ldquo;fashioned from <em>Tritia gibbosula</em> shells,&rdquo; suggesting consistency rather than heterogeneity. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the beads&rsquo; &ldquo;expediency,&rdquo; or convenience or practicality. The text doesn&rsquo;t support the idea of convenience (there&rsquo;s no indication, for example, that the beads were easy to use or make) but rather focuses on the beads&rsquo; abundant presence at MSA sites. Choice D is incorrect. While &ldquo;fecundity&rdquo; can suggest abundant production, it specifically refers to fertility, and the text doesn&rsquo;t connect the beads to this theme. Instead, the text attributes their widespread presence to the development of symbolic behavior in humans and the natural availability of <em>Tritia gibbosula</em> during the MSA.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771170,"updateDate":1776362771170}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771170, 1776362771170),
    ($SATQ$bce627d9$SATQ$, $SATQ$f0d86904-3056-437a-b590-5363205a9d0d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$eeab4166-b5f8-439a-9cd2-4a62403843df$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Mineralogical differences are detectable in samples collected from two locations on the near-Earth asteroid Ryugu, but such differences may not indicate substantial compositional variations in the asteroid. Cosmochemist Kazuhide Nagashima and colleagues note that at the small scale of the samples, the distribution of minerals is unlikely to be <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a731ccf7-33a0-4f50-966c-5eac8cdd8ce2","content":"<p>neglected</p>"},{"id":"e6d16305-e7d0-44d5-a974-ba142f8b13c3","content":"<p>redundant</p>"},{"id":"b1f78083-2383-4c82-bcb2-e83bd681ad19","content":"<p>ongoing</p>"},{"id":"b1405d12-138c-4e83-8438-c7765d12f650","content":"<p>uniform</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["b1405d12-138c-4e83-8438-c7765d12f650"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The text tells us that the samples are too "small scale" to reflect the composition of the asteroid, which probably doesn&rsquo;t show the same variation on a large scale. This suggests that the mineral composition of the samples are unlikely to be exactly the same from sample to sample.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. "Neglected" means "suffering a lack of proper care" or "abandoned," which doesn&rsquo;t work here. The text never suggests that the distribution of minerals in the samples would be neglected, so this statement doesn&rsquo;t logically follow. Choice B is incorrect. "Redundant" means "not or no longer useful or needed," which is too strong. The text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the variation between the samples isn&rsquo;t a useful finding at all&mdash;just that we can&rsquo;t assume that the large-scale composition of the asteroid will show the same variation. But the composition of the samples might be useful for something else. Choice C is incorrect. "Ongoing" means "still in progress," which doesn&rsquo;t make sense: the distribution of minerals in a sample can&rsquo;t be "ongoing."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-WIC-M004$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$52ecad5e-c281-4016-8b92-77ead9d5e08b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"bce627d9","external_id":"f0d86904-3056-437a-b590-5363205a9d0d","vaultid":"eeab4166-b5f8-439a-9cd2-4a62403843df","uId":"c9fb230e-d60f-4d41-9e46-9beb2b11d31d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Mineralogical differences are detectable in samples collected from two locations on the near-Earth asteroid Ryugu, but such differences may not indicate substantial compositional variations in the asteroid. Cosmochemist Kazuhide Nagashima and colleagues note that at the small scale of the samples, the distribution of minerals is unlikely to be <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a731ccf7-33a0-4f50-966c-5eac8cdd8ce2","content":"<p>neglected</p>"},{"id":"e6d16305-e7d0-44d5-a974-ba142f8b13c3","content":"<p>redundant</p>"},{"id":"b1f78083-2383-4c82-bcb2-e83bd681ad19","content":"<p>ongoing</p>"},{"id":"b1405d12-138c-4e83-8438-c7765d12f650","content":"<p>uniform</p>"}],"keys":["b1405d12-138c-4e83-8438-c7765d12f650"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The text tells us that the samples are too \"small scale\" to reflect the composition of the asteroid, which probably doesn&rsquo;t show the same variation on a large scale. This suggests that the mineral composition of the samples are unlikely to be exactly the same from sample to sample.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. \"Neglected\" means \"suffering a lack of proper care\" or \"abandoned,\" which doesn&rsquo;t work here. The text never suggests that the distribution of minerals in the samples would be neglected, so this statement doesn&rsquo;t logically follow. Choice B is incorrect. \"Redundant\" means \"not or no longer useful or needed,\" which is too strong. The text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the variation between the samples isn&rsquo;t a useful finding at all&mdash;just that we can&rsquo;t assume that the large-scale composition of the asteroid will show the same variation. But the composition of the samples might be useful for something else. Choice C is incorrect. \"Ongoing\" means \"still in progress,\" which doesn&rsquo;t make sense: the distribution of minerals in a sample can&rsquo;t be \"ongoing.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-WIC-M004","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"52ecad5e-c281-4016-8b92-77ead9d5e08b","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959378,"updateDate":1691007959378}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959378, 1691007959378),
    ($SATQ$bdab32fc$SATQ$, $SATQ$93e5ee6c-f243-4144-b2d4-35213665d0e7$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$a52fd840-160c-4544-ab45-be9359535194$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Cucurbits, a group of plants that includes squash and melons, relied on mastodons to spread their seeds in the Ice Age. When these animals died out, cucurbits faced extinction in turn, having lost their means of seed dispersal. Around this time, however, the ancestors of Indigenous peoples in North America began raising cucurbits as crops, thus <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the plants&rsquo; survival.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d4fde03d-d682-4f1f-bcde-2a2214ff1205","content":"<p>verifying</p>"},{"id":"6a60ed46-1049-4db7-a2c0-c9353be49394","content":"<p>multiplying</p>"},{"id":"9fd71a9d-28a7-49e8-a6c3-69fa0d305a95","content":"<p>comforting</p>"},{"id":"4532ebaf-1d6e-439e-a9de-e861ec5876ac","content":"<p>ensuring</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4532ebaf-1d6e-439e-a9de-e861ec5876ac"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of cucurbits. In this context, &ldquo;ensuring&rdquo; means guaranteeing, or making sure of, the cucurbits&rsquo; survival. The text states that cucurbits faced extinction in the past because their means of seed dispersal disappeared, but the ancestors of Indigenous peoples in North America began farming cucurbits around that same time, so the crops were no longer threatened. Therefore, the context supports the idea that the ancestors of Indigenous peoples in North America helped with ensuring the cucurbits&rsquo; survival. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context verifying means making sure that something is accurate. In the text, the ancestors of Indigenous peoples in North America were ensuring the survival, not the accuracy of, the cucurbits. Choice B is incorrect. Although the cucurbit crops themselves were multiplying, or growing in number, as a result of the work of the ancestors of Indigenous peoples in North America, it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context to say that the survival of the plants was multiplying. Choice C is incorrect because according to the text, in raising cucurbits as crops, the ancestors of Indigenous peoples in North America were attempting to help the plants grow and survive; they weren&rsquo;t attempting to comfort, or free the plants from pain. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-057-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$012c3e66-ee3f-4005-9849-1a52c4c3584a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"bdab32fc","external_id":"93e5ee6c-f243-4144-b2d4-35213665d0e7","vaultid":"a52fd840-160c-4544-ab45-be9359535194","uId":"5c37176a-0f11-486a-b166-c786e64c61f0","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Cucurbits, a group of plants that includes squash and melons, relied on mastodons to spread their seeds in the Ice Age. When these animals died out, cucurbits faced extinction in turn, having lost their means of seed dispersal. Around this time, however, the ancestors of Indigenous peoples in North America began raising cucurbits as crops, thus <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the plants&rsquo; survival.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d4fde03d-d682-4f1f-bcde-2a2214ff1205","content":"<p>verifying</p>"},{"id":"6a60ed46-1049-4db7-a2c0-c9353be49394","content":"<p>multiplying</p>"},{"id":"9fd71a9d-28a7-49e8-a6c3-69fa0d305a95","content":"<p>comforting</p>"},{"id":"4532ebaf-1d6e-439e-a9de-e861ec5876ac","content":"<p>ensuring</p>"}],"keys":["4532ebaf-1d6e-439e-a9de-e861ec5876ac"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of cucurbits. In this context, &ldquo;ensuring&rdquo; means guaranteeing, or making sure of, the cucurbits&rsquo; survival. The text states that cucurbits faced extinction in the past because their means of seed dispersal disappeared, but the ancestors of Indigenous peoples in North America began farming cucurbits around that same time, so the crops were no longer threatened. Therefore, the context supports the idea that the ancestors of Indigenous peoples in North America helped with ensuring the cucurbits&rsquo; survival. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context verifying means making sure that something is accurate. In the text, the ancestors of Indigenous peoples in North America were ensuring the survival, not the accuracy of, the cucurbits. Choice B is incorrect. Although the cucurbit crops themselves were multiplying, or growing in number, as a result of the work of the ancestors of Indigenous peoples in North America, it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context to say that the survival of the plants was multiplying. Choice C is incorrect because according to the text, in raising cucurbits as crops, the ancestors of Indigenous peoples in North America were attempting to help the plants grow and survive; they weren&rsquo;t attempting to comfort, or free the plants from pain. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-057-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"012c3e66-ee3f-4005-9849-1a52c4c3584a","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959325,"updateDate":1691007959325}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959325, 1691007959325),
    ($SATQ$be612a26$SATQ$, $SATQ$d7c11206-1d75-4efc-b61f-5619e10f2743$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Recent measurements of the mass of the <em>W </em>boson (a subatomic particle) were notable not only for the mere fact that the particle&rsquo;s mass differed from expectations but for the <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> of that difference: the measured mass of the <em>W </em>boson was seven standard deviations higher than predicted by the standard model of particle physics.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0643777e-5971-469f-b3a4-82f5ff840016","content":"<p>cause</p>"},{"id":"cc003941-2dda-4d88-8cc3-607c045d5ad2","content":"<p>existence</p>"},{"id":"38cd1171-2ee2-45b8-85b1-09320b7620be","content":"<p>implication</p>"},{"id":"d5bafe45-8c42-4922-b98f-5eef40f4bdd8","content":"<p>scale</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["d5bafe45-8c42-4922-b98f-5eef40f4bdd8"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the <em>W</em> boson. In this context, "scale" means size or extent. The text indicates that recent measurements of the <em>W</em> boson were remarkable because they revealed the subatomic particle&rsquo;s mass to be much greater ("seven standard deviations higher") than had been expected based on a standard model. This context conveys not only that the measurements differed from predictions but also that the extent of the difference was very large.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text indicates that there was a very large difference between the predicted mass and the actual measured mass of the subatomic particle, it doesn&rsquo;t explain the cause of, or reason behind, that difference. Choice B is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that the measurements of the subatomic particle were notable because they differed from expectations and also because a difference existed between the measurements and what had been predicted; the text would be repeating the idea that there was a difference instead of adding to that idea. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t convey any implications of, or consequences of or conclusions drawn from, the fact that the mass of the subatomic particle differed from expectations; the context indicates only that the actual measured mass was much higher than the predicted mass.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"be612a26","external_id":"d7c11206-1d75-4efc-b61f-5619e10f2743","vaultid":null,"uId":"0d959665-84fa-4d8c-83f3-70c93b2a47e4","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Recent measurements of the mass of the <em>W </em>boson (a subatomic particle) were notable not only for the mere fact that the particle&rsquo;s mass differed from expectations but for the <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> of that difference: the measured mass of the <em>W </em>boson was seven standard deviations higher than predicted by the standard model of particle physics.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0643777e-5971-469f-b3a4-82f5ff840016","content":"<p>cause</p>"},{"id":"cc003941-2dda-4d88-8cc3-607c045d5ad2","content":"<p>existence</p>"},{"id":"38cd1171-2ee2-45b8-85b1-09320b7620be","content":"<p>implication</p>"},{"id":"d5bafe45-8c42-4922-b98f-5eef40f4bdd8","content":"<p>scale</p>"}],"keys":["d5bafe45-8c42-4922-b98f-5eef40f4bdd8"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the <em>W</em> boson. In this context, \"scale\" means size or extent. The text indicates that recent measurements of the <em>W</em> boson were remarkable because they revealed the subatomic particle&rsquo;s mass to be much greater (\"seven standard deviations higher\") than had been expected based on a standard model. This context conveys not only that the measurements differed from predictions but also that the extent of the difference was very large.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text indicates that there was a very large difference between the predicted mass and the actual measured mass of the subatomic particle, it doesn&rsquo;t explain the cause of, or reason behind, that difference. Choice B is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that the measurements of the subatomic particle were notable because they differed from expectations and also because a difference existed between the measurements and what had been predicted; the text would be repeating the idea that there was a difference instead of adding to that idea. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t convey any implications of, or consequences of or conclusions drawn from, the fact that the mass of the subatomic particle differed from expectations; the context indicates only that the actual measured mass was much higher than the predicted mass.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323052,"updateDate":1730147323052}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323052, 1730147323052),
    ($SATQ$be947479$SATQ$, $SATQ$4df2302b-b51f-452c-89ac-da7978924c29$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Jazz tap is a dance form that was first developed in African American communities. Jazz tap was heavily influenced by jazz music, which became widely popular in the United States in the 1920s. Tap dancers were inspired by jazz music&rsquo;s quick rhythms and by the way jazz musicians would make up melodies as they played. As jazz music continued to develop in the 1930s and 1940s, jazz tap evolved with it. Because of jazz music&rsquo;s influence, jazz tap quickly developed into a dance form that was very different from earlier kinds of tap dance.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0ad1595c-c3ed-4aa6-8ecc-65ba241cbac9","content":"<p>It explains why audiences prefer some kinds of music over others.</p>"},{"id":"aef780f0-f30f-48ea-b249-419fb98a0f19","content":"<p>It discusses the development of a dance form.</p>"},{"id":"fe4e5dc8-8f56-403e-811f-5f748d5328b3","content":"<p>It describes how to play a musical instrument.</p>"},{"id":"5d211800-99fa-4469-85c2-623b217f3eb2","content":"<p>It emphasizes the popularity of a famous dancer.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["aef780f0-f30f-48ea-b249-419fb98a0f19"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it best describes the main purpose of the text. The text indicates where and when jazz tap first developed (in African American communities in the 1920s) and what influenced it (the quick rhythms and improvisations in jazz music) and then explains that it evolved alongside jazz music in the 1930s and 1940s, resulting in a very different form of tap dance than had existed before. Therefore, the main purpose of the text is to discuss jazz tap&rsquo;s development.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text indicates that jazz music became widely popular in the US in the 1920s and describes some of jazz music&rsquo;s qualities, the text never explains why audiences prefer some kinds of music&mdash;jazz or otherwise&mdash;over others. Choice C is incorrect because the text never mentions any musical instruments and doesn&rsquo;t describe how to play one. Choice D is incorrect because the text discusses jazz tap generally and never identifies a particular dancer, famous or otherwise.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"be947479","external_id":"4df2302b-b51f-452c-89ac-da7978924c29","vaultid":null,"uId":"ca5b1d5f-300e-4c93-99ab-c2dde2f1efd5","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Jazz tap is a dance form that was first developed in African American communities. Jazz tap was heavily influenced by jazz music, which became widely popular in the United States in the 1920s. Tap dancers were inspired by jazz music&rsquo;s quick rhythms and by the way jazz musicians would make up melodies as they played. As jazz music continued to develop in the 1930s and 1940s, jazz tap evolved with it. Because of jazz music&rsquo;s influence, jazz tap quickly developed into a dance form that was very different from earlier kinds of tap dance.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0ad1595c-c3ed-4aa6-8ecc-65ba241cbac9","content":"<p>It explains why audiences prefer some kinds of music over others.</p>"},{"id":"aef780f0-f30f-48ea-b249-419fb98a0f19","content":"<p>It discusses the development of a dance form.</p>"},{"id":"fe4e5dc8-8f56-403e-811f-5f748d5328b3","content":"<p>It describes how to play a musical instrument.</p>"},{"id":"5d211800-99fa-4469-85c2-623b217f3eb2","content":"<p>It emphasizes the popularity of a famous dancer.</p>"}],"keys":["aef780f0-f30f-48ea-b249-419fb98a0f19"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it best describes the main purpose of the text. The text indicates where and when jazz tap first developed (in African American communities in the 1920s) and what influenced it (the quick rhythms and improvisations in jazz music) and then explains that it evolved alongside jazz music in the 1930s and 1940s, resulting in a very different form of tap dance than had existed before. Therefore, the main purpose of the text is to discuss jazz tap&rsquo;s development.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text indicates that jazz music became widely popular in the US in the 1920s and describes some of jazz music&rsquo;s qualities, the text never explains why audiences prefer some kinds of music&mdash;jazz or otherwise&mdash;over others. Choice C is incorrect because the text never mentions any musical instruments and doesn&rsquo;t describe how to play one. Choice D is incorrect because the text discusses jazz tap generally and never identifies a particular dancer, famous or otherwise.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669206,"updateDate":1743430669206}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669206, 1743430669206),
    ($SATQ$be94c9fc$SATQ$, $SATQ$cc4c76e5-535b-4376-baf9-3c6a772c0cf3$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from James Baldwin&rsquo;s 1956 novel <em>Giovanni&rsquo;s Room</em>. The narrator is riding in a taxi down a street lined with food vendors and shoppers in Paris, France.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The multitude of Paris seems to be dressed in blue every day but Sunday, when, for the most part, they put on an unbelievably festive black. Here they were now, in blue, <span style="text-decoration: underline;" role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content">disputing</span>, every inch, our passage, with their wagons, handtrucks, their bursting baskets carried at an angle steeply self-confident on the back.</p>
</blockquote>
<p style="text-align: right;"><span class="dap_copyright">&copy;1956 by James Baldwin</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;disputing&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ada26ef9-55bb-462d-9865-0034e4a689fb","content":"<p>Arguing about</p>"},{"id":"b78912bd-adb3-4417-8f71-a97820099988","content":"<p>Disapproving of</p>"},{"id":"e15d63f1-f3a3-4ff2-948a-ec3d551981fd","content":"<p>Asserting possession of</p>"},{"id":"753db0a8-e07b-4228-922c-4473ddad51df","content":"<p>Providing resistance to</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["753db0a8-e07b-4228-922c-4473ddad51df"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because as used in the text, "disputing" most nearly means providing resistance to. The narrator is in a taxi as it drives down a street lined with so many food vendors and shoppers that the narrator describes them as "the multitude of Paris," meaning an immense group of people. The street is essentially a large open-air market, and there are so many people pushing small wagons of goods and carrying shopping baskets that "every inch" of the taxi&rsquo;s progress is impeded. In other words, the people are providing resistance to the taxi&rsquo;s attempt to drive down the street.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although in some contexts, "disputing" can mean arguing, the narrator doesn&rsquo;t portray the shoppers and vendors as arguing with the driver of the taxi or, indeed, arguing at all. Choice B is incorrect. Although in some contexts, "disputing" can mean expressing disapproval, the narrator doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the shoppers and vendors necessarily disapprove of the taxi&rsquo;s attempt to drive down the street. Instead, their combined presence along the street has the effect of impeding the taxi&rsquo;s progress. Choice C is incorrect because, as the narrator explains, both the multitude of people and the taxi are using a public space (a street) at the same time. The narrator doesn&rsquo;t go so far as to suggest that the people feel that they, and not the taxi, possess exclusive access to the street.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"be94c9fc","external_id":"cc4c76e5-535b-4376-baf9-3c6a772c0cf3","vaultid":null,"uId":"40bd5e04-6e41-4b8f-8f1d-06d0ae6b3109","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from James Baldwin&rsquo;s 1956 novel <em>Giovanni&rsquo;s Room</em>. The narrator is riding in a taxi down a street lined with food vendors and shoppers in Paris, France.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The multitude of Paris seems to be dressed in blue every day but Sunday, when, for the most part, they put on an unbelievably festive black. Here they were now, in blue, <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\">disputing</span>, every inch, our passage, with their wagons, handtrucks, their bursting baskets carried at an angle steeply self-confident on the back.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p style=\"text-align: right;\"><span class=\"dap_copyright\">&copy;1956 by James Baldwin</span></p>","stem":"<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;disputing&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ada26ef9-55bb-462d-9865-0034e4a689fb","content":"<p>Arguing about</p>"},{"id":"b78912bd-adb3-4417-8f71-a97820099988","content":"<p>Disapproving of</p>"},{"id":"e15d63f1-f3a3-4ff2-948a-ec3d551981fd","content":"<p>Asserting possession of</p>"},{"id":"753db0a8-e07b-4228-922c-4473ddad51df","content":"<p>Providing resistance to</p>"}],"keys":["753db0a8-e07b-4228-922c-4473ddad51df"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because as used in the text, \"disputing\" most nearly means providing resistance to. The narrator is in a taxi as it drives down a street lined with so many food vendors and shoppers that the narrator describes them as \"the multitude of Paris,\" meaning an immense group of people. The street is essentially a large open-air market, and there are so many people pushing small wagons of goods and carrying shopping baskets that \"every inch\" of the taxi&rsquo;s progress is impeded. In other words, the people are providing resistance to the taxi&rsquo;s attempt to drive down the street.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although in some contexts, \"disputing\" can mean arguing, the narrator doesn&rsquo;t portray the shoppers and vendors as arguing with the driver of the taxi or, indeed, arguing at all. Choice B is incorrect. Although in some contexts, \"disputing\" can mean expressing disapproval, the narrator doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the shoppers and vendors necessarily disapprove of the taxi&rsquo;s attempt to drive down the street. Instead, their combined presence along the street has the effect of impeding the taxi&rsquo;s progress. Choice C is incorrect because, as the narrator explains, both the multitude of people and the taxi are using a public space (a street) at the same time. The narrator doesn&rsquo;t go so far as to suggest that the people feel that they, and not the taxi, possess exclusive access to the street.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323052,"updateDate":1730147323052}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323052, 1730147323052),
    ($SATQ$c0e1b70a$SATQ$, $SATQ$eb3dda15-c27a-4917-8be4-98b64ffb032a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$8f4a209a-4acc-4fa4-890d-9ae2fb35244a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from Etsu Inagaki Sugimoto&rsquo;s 1925 memoir <em>A Daughter of the Samurai</em>. As a young woman, Sugimoto moved from feudal Japan to the United States.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The standards of my own and my adopted country differed so widely in some ways, and my love for both lands was so sincere, that sometimes I had an odd feeling of standing upon a cloud in space, and gazing with measuring eyes upon two separate worlds. At first I was continually trying to explain, by Japanese standards, all the queer things that came every day before my surprised eyes; for no one seemed to know the origin or significance of even the most familiar customs, nor why they existed and were followed.</p>
</blockquote>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"729be0ea-f19e-4ecb-a45e-979222985e58","content":"<p>To convey the narrator&rsquo;s experience of observing and making sense of differences between two cultures she embraces</p>"},{"id":"0657c7ed-f5e4-42c7-bac5-17ba5e0afef0","content":"<p>To establish the narrator&rsquo;s hope of forming connections with new companions by sharing customs she learned as a child</p>"},{"id":"e0fcbbcd-227d-4ecf-b5ba-e393bca84ede","content":"<p>To reveal the narrator&rsquo;s recognition that she is hesitant to ask questions about certain aspects of a culture she is newly encountering&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"634dd971-29b9-4a85-9ec1-83b9391166e5","content":"<p>To emphasize the narrator&rsquo;s wonder at discovering that the physical distance between two countries is greater than she had expected</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["729be0ea-f19e-4ecb-a45e-979222985e58"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most accurately describes the main purpose of the text. The narrator asserts that she loves both her &ldquo;own&rdquo; country (Japan) and her &ldquo;adopted country&rdquo; (the United States) even though the two countries differ &ldquo;widely.&rdquo; She also indicates that, at first, she would try to explain unfamiliar experiences that she had in the United States using the standards ingrained in her from growing up in Japan. Thus, the main purpose of the text is to convey the narrator&rsquo;s experience of observing and making sense of the differences between two cultures she embraces. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text makes no reference to possible companions. Although the text does indicate that the narrator sometimes used the cultural framework she acquired growing up in Japan to explain some experiences she&rsquo;s had, there is no suggestion that this was in service of making friends. And although &ldquo;no one seemed to know&rdquo; strongly implies that the narrator has interacted with other people in the United States, there is no indication that these conversations involved her discussing Japanese customs. Choice C is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that the narrator was hesitant to ask questions. In fact, the narrator indicates that &ldquo;no one seemed to know the origin&rdquo; of various customs, which provides evidence that, rather than being hesitant, she sought information from several people. Choice D is incorrect because the text makes no reference to the physical distance between Japan and the United States. Although the narrator indicates that the two countries differ &ldquo;widely&rdquo; and likens them to &ldquo;two separate worlds,&rdquo; these descriptions relate to cultural aspects of the countries and the narrator&rsquo;s feelings about the two countries, not the physical distance between them. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-027-TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$89bce1ad-8a5b-4803-9081-5ba35840fef7$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"c0e1b70a","external_id":"eb3dda15-c27a-4917-8be4-98b64ffb032a","vaultid":"8f4a209a-4acc-4fa4-890d-9ae2fb35244a","uId":"8e664921-823b-4d03-a43c-670dc18d0e90","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from Etsu Inagaki Sugimoto&rsquo;s 1925 memoir <em>A Daughter of the Samurai</em>. As a young woman, Sugimoto moved from feudal Japan to the United States.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The standards of my own and my adopted country differed so widely in some ways, and my love for both lands was so sincere, that sometimes I had an odd feeling of standing upon a cloud in space, and gazing with measuring eyes upon two separate worlds. At first I was continually trying to explain, by Japanese standards, all the queer things that came every day before my surprised eyes; for no one seemed to know the origin or significance of even the most familiar customs, nor why they existed and were followed.</p>\n</blockquote>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"729be0ea-f19e-4ecb-a45e-979222985e58","content":"<p>To convey the narrator&rsquo;s experience of observing and making sense of differences between two cultures she embraces</p>"},{"id":"0657c7ed-f5e4-42c7-bac5-17ba5e0afef0","content":"<p>To establish the narrator&rsquo;s hope of forming connections with new companions by sharing customs she learned as a child</p>"},{"id":"e0fcbbcd-227d-4ecf-b5ba-e393bca84ede","content":"<p>To reveal the narrator&rsquo;s recognition that she is hesitant to ask questions about certain aspects of a culture she is newly encountering&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"634dd971-29b9-4a85-9ec1-83b9391166e5","content":"<p>To emphasize the narrator&rsquo;s wonder at discovering that the physical distance between two countries is greater than she had expected</p>"}],"keys":["729be0ea-f19e-4ecb-a45e-979222985e58"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most accurately describes the main purpose of the text. The narrator asserts that she loves both her &ldquo;own&rdquo; country (Japan) and her &ldquo;adopted country&rdquo; (the United States) even though the two countries differ &ldquo;widely.&rdquo; She also indicates that, at first, she would try to explain unfamiliar experiences that she had in the United States using the standards ingrained in her from growing up in Japan. Thus, the main purpose of the text is to convey the narrator&rsquo;s experience of observing and making sense of the differences between two cultures she embraces. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text makes no reference to possible companions. Although the text does indicate that the narrator sometimes used the cultural framework she acquired growing up in Japan to explain some experiences she&rsquo;s had, there is no suggestion that this was in service of making friends. And although &ldquo;no one seemed to know&rdquo; strongly implies that the narrator has interacted with other people in the United States, there is no indication that these conversations involved her discussing Japanese customs. Choice C is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that the narrator was hesitant to ask questions. In fact, the narrator indicates that &ldquo;no one seemed to know the origin&rdquo; of various customs, which provides evidence that, rather than being hesitant, she sought information from several people. Choice D is incorrect because the text makes no reference to the physical distance between Japan and the United States. Although the narrator indicates that the two countries differ &ldquo;widely&rdquo; and likens them to &ldquo;two separate worlds,&rdquo; these descriptions relate to cultural aspects of the countries and the narrator&rsquo;s feelings about the two countries, not the physical distance between them. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-027-TSP","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"89bce1ad-8a5b-4803-9081-5ba35840fef7","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959318,"updateDate":1691007959318}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959318, 1691007959318),
    ($SATQ$c106b9f7$SATQ$, $SATQ$3edde485-3d30-41b8-8af4-98ffa91a318a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$b7ff6596-4cdb-4e80-b44f-520c92e2d012$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Cross-Text Connections$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><strong>Text 1</strong></p>
<p>American sculptor Edmonia Lewis is best known for her sculptures that represent figures from history and mythology, such as <em>The Death of Cleopatra</em> and <em>Haga</em>r. Although Lewis sculpted other subjects, her career as a sculptor is best represented by the works in which she depicted these historical and mythical themes.&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong>Text 2&nbsp;</strong></p>
<p>Art historians have typically ignored the many portrait busts Edmonia Lewis created. Lewis likely carved these busts (sculptures of a person&rsquo;s head) frequently throughout her long career. She is known for her sculptures that represent historical figures, but Lewis likely supported herself financially by carving portrait busts for acquaintances who paid her to represent their features. Thus, Lewis&rsquo;s portrait busts are a central aspect of her career as a sculptor.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the texts, both authors would most likely agree with which statement?&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"e5aff14c-f483-4952-8188-f8d56826b820","content":"<p>Lewis&rsquo;s portrait busts have overshadowed her other work.</p>"},{"id":"82efa7ef-9b6f-48d4-be27-09e98ffe3ba5","content":"<p><em>The Death of Cleopatra</em> is Lewis&rsquo;s most famous piece.</p>"},{"id":"9af44078-f7b4-489f-a913-87534d138df0","content":"<p>Sculpting representations of historical figures was a short-lived trend.</p>"},{"id":"6ffde011-05c6-487a-9eb5-29e5efe4a87c","content":"<p>Lewis&rsquo;s works are varied in the subjects they depict.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6ffde011-05c6-487a-9eb5-29e5efe4a87c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. Author 1 acknowledges that Lewis sculpted other subjects besides historical and mythical figures, suggesting a variety of subjects depicted. Author 2 mentions that Lewis carved portrait busts as well as historical sculptures, which also implies variation among Lewis&rsquo;s subjects. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Neither text suggests that Lewis&rsquo;s portrait busts have received more attention or appreciation than her other work. Author 1 briefly mentions her &ldquo;other works,&rdquo; but mostly focuses on her historical and mythical works. Author 2 states that art historians have typically ignored her portrait busts, which suggests that they haven&rsquo;t overshadowed her other work. Choice B is incorrect. Neither text explicitly states that <em>The Death of Cleopatra</em> is Lewis&rsquo;s most famous piece. Author 1 mentions it as one example of her historical works, but does not single it out as being more important or influential than <em>Hagar</em>. Author 2 does not mention it at all, focusing instead on her portrait busts. Choice C is incorrect. This choice isn&rsquo;t supported by the texts. Neither text suggests that sculpting historical figures was a trend that faded quickly. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-CTC-E006$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$1bb96b18-733c-4375-aaaa-157395a89bcc$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"c106b9f7","external_id":"3edde485-3d30-41b8-8af4-98ffa91a318a","vaultid":"b7ff6596-4cdb-4e80-b44f-520c92e2d012","uId":"e44df05d-051e-4c5c-946e-419379b66608","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"CTC","skill_desc":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><strong>Text 1</strong></p>\n<p>American sculptor Edmonia Lewis is best known for her sculptures that represent figures from history and mythology, such as <em>The Death of Cleopatra</em> and <em>Haga</em>r. Although Lewis sculpted other subjects, her career as a sculptor is best represented by the works in which she depicted these historical and mythical themes.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Text 2&nbsp;</strong></p>\n<p>Art historians have typically ignored the many portrait busts Edmonia Lewis created. Lewis likely carved these busts (sculptures of a person&rsquo;s head) frequently throughout her long career. She is known for her sculptures that represent historical figures, but Lewis likely supported herself financially by carving portrait busts for acquaintances who paid her to represent their features. Thus, Lewis&rsquo;s portrait busts are a central aspect of her career as a sculptor.</p>","stem":"<p>Based on the texts, both authors would most likely agree with which statement?&nbsp;</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"e5aff14c-f483-4952-8188-f8d56826b820","content":"<p>Lewis&rsquo;s portrait busts have overshadowed her other work.</p>"},{"id":"82efa7ef-9b6f-48d4-be27-09e98ffe3ba5","content":"<p><em>The Death of Cleopatra</em> is Lewis&rsquo;s most famous piece.</p>"},{"id":"9af44078-f7b4-489f-a913-87534d138df0","content":"<p>Sculpting representations of historical figures was a short-lived trend.</p>"},{"id":"6ffde011-05c6-487a-9eb5-29e5efe4a87c","content":"<p>Lewis&rsquo;s works are varied in the subjects they depict.</p>"}],"keys":["6ffde011-05c6-487a-9eb5-29e5efe4a87c"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. Author 1 acknowledges that Lewis sculpted other subjects besides historical and mythical figures, suggesting a variety of subjects depicted. Author 2 mentions that Lewis carved portrait busts as well as historical sculptures, which also implies variation among Lewis&rsquo;s subjects. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Neither text suggests that Lewis&rsquo;s portrait busts have received more attention or appreciation than her other work. Author 1 briefly mentions her &ldquo;other works,&rdquo; but mostly focuses on her historical and mythical works. Author 2 states that art historians have typically ignored her portrait busts, which suggests that they haven&rsquo;t overshadowed her other work. Choice B is incorrect. Neither text explicitly states that <em>The Death of Cleopatra</em> is Lewis&rsquo;s most famous piece. Author 1 mentions it as one example of her historical works, but does not single it out as being more important or influential than <em>Hagar</em>. Author 2 does not mention it at all, focusing instead on her portrait busts. Choice C is incorrect. This choice isn&rsquo;t supported by the texts. Neither text suggests that sculpting historical figures was a trend that faded quickly. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-CTC-E006","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"1bb96b18-733c-4375-aaaa-157395a89bcc","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959361,"updateDate":1691007959361}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959361, 1691007959361),
    ($SATQ$c14daa3c$SATQ$, $SATQ$edd110d7-6707-4876-9f5f-dfa02104e47f$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$1839c7db-82cd-4c00-81db-c712b64db688$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Close analysis of the painting <em>Girl with a Flute, </em>long attributed to the seventeenth-century Dutch painter Johannes Vermeer, has revealed subtle deviations from the artist&rsquo;s signature techniques. These variations suggest that the work may be that of a student under Vermeer&rsquo;s tutelage&mdash;potentially <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> our understanding of Vermeer as a solitary artist.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"40dd06d3-0a5e-4fb1-9a8c-3a523da3fd64","content":"<p>negating&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"28e1689b-77d5-489a-915a-7eb4263a7a21","content":"<p>prefiguring</p>"},{"id":"f45cb36d-de28-454e-87f5-9405148640a8","content":"<p>entrenching</p>"},{"id":"4687ed61-2274-47ee-bb96-be73064545ba","content":"<p>substantiating</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["40dd06d3-0a5e-4fb1-9a8c-3a523da3fd64"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. "Negating" means "reversing" or "making invalid." Proving that Vermeer worked with students would reverse the view of him as a solitary artist.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. "Prefiguring" means "being an early indicator of." There already existed views of Vermeer as a solitary painter, so a new painting would not be an early indicator of those views. Rather, a painting proving that Vermeer had a student would contradict those earlier views. Choice C is incorrect. "Entrenching" means "solidifying." A painting proving that Vermeer had a student would not solidify views of him as solitary, but would rather contradict those views. Choice D is incorrect. "Substantiating" means "supporting with proof." A painting proving that Vermeer had a student would not support views of him as solitary, but would rather contradict those views.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-WIC-H007$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$70420315-40c6-43c0-ba6b-8a3cc71a5cc7$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"c14daa3c","external_id":"edd110d7-6707-4876-9f5f-dfa02104e47f","vaultid":"1839c7db-82cd-4c00-81db-c712b64db688","uId":"7c548114-fee8-45f8-8bbf-1da0b87ca274","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Close analysis of the painting <em>Girl with a Flute, </em>long attributed to the seventeenth-century Dutch painter Johannes Vermeer, has revealed subtle deviations from the artist&rsquo;s signature techniques. These variations suggest that the work may be that of a student under Vermeer&rsquo;s tutelage&mdash;potentially <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> our understanding of Vermeer as a solitary artist.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"40dd06d3-0a5e-4fb1-9a8c-3a523da3fd64","content":"<p>negating&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"28e1689b-77d5-489a-915a-7eb4263a7a21","content":"<p>prefiguring</p>"},{"id":"f45cb36d-de28-454e-87f5-9405148640a8","content":"<p>entrenching</p>"},{"id":"4687ed61-2274-47ee-bb96-be73064545ba","content":"<p>substantiating</p>"}],"keys":["40dd06d3-0a5e-4fb1-9a8c-3a523da3fd64"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. \"Negating\" means \"reversing\" or \"making invalid.\" Proving that Vermeer worked with students would reverse the view of him as a solitary artist.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. \"Prefiguring\" means \"being an early indicator of.\" There already existed views of Vermeer as a solitary painter, so a new painting would not be an early indicator of those views. Rather, a painting proving that Vermeer had a student would contradict those earlier views. Choice C is incorrect. \"Entrenching\" means \"solidifying.\" A painting proving that Vermeer had a student would not solidify views of him as solitary, but would rather contradict those views. Choice D is incorrect. \"Substantiating\" means \"supporting with proof.\" A painting proving that Vermeer had a student would not support views of him as solitary, but would rather contradict those views.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-WIC-H007","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"70420315-40c6-43c0-ba6b-8a3cc71a5cc7","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959377,"updateDate":1691007959377}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959377, 1691007959377),
    ($SATQ$c19b2f77$SATQ$, $SATQ$75d0401c-75d9-4070-be56-daf441f2908a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Cross-Text Connections$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><span class="paired_passage" role="heading" aria-level="2"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>
<p>Little is known about how plate tectonics&mdash;wherein slabs of Earth&rsquo;s crust move over, under, away from, and against one another&mdash;began. <span style="text-decoration: underline;" role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content">Some researchers contend</span> that tectonic movements began around 3 billion years ago, often noting that computer models of Earth&rsquo;s mantle temperature at the time indicate that the mantle would have been sufficiently molten to enable the plates to move.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span class="paired_passage" role="heading" aria-level="2"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>
<p>Ultimately, any plausible claim about the inception of tectonic movement must rest on empirical evidence from the geological record. Researcher Wriju Chowdhury and his team analyzed the geochemistry of zircon crystals to gain insight into the chemical composition of the magma from which the crystals formed and, based on the data, compellingly argue that plate tectonics may have been occurring as early as 4.2 billion years ago.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to what &ldquo;some researchers contend&rdquo; as described in Text 1?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"368bbf12-3876-486c-a831-89650d86f302","content":"<p>By suggesting that the temperature of Earth&rsquo;s mantle 3 billion years ago was likely insufficient to allow for the level of tectonic movement predicted by computer models</p>"},{"id":"c60e5c6e-9b40-4ea2-9eee-82be9753f271","content":"<p>By distinguishing between computer models of Earth&rsquo;s mantle temperature that reliably predict the onset of plate tectonics and those that do not</p>"},{"id":"a14e3807-1d3c-47bf-908c-86f496ba2777","content":"<p>By indicating that computer models of Earth&rsquo;s mantle temperature are still being improved such that new models tend to be much more reliable than their predecessors</p>"},{"id":"cf64fc36-831a-42eb-a70c-f25a58af6ae4","content":"<p>By asserting that a more definitive form of evidence than the computer models suggests a different timeline for the onset of plate tectonics on Earth</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["cf64fc36-831a-42eb-a70c-f25a58af6ae4"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it reflects how the author of Text 2 would most likely respond to what the researchers mentioned in Text 1 contend. Text 1 discusses the lack of knowledge of how plate tectonics on Earth began. Text 1 also mentions researchers who contend that movements of tectonic plates began around 3 billion years ago. As support for this assertion, these researchers cite computer models (which are simulations, not empirical evidence) of the temperature in Earth&rsquo;s mantle that show that at that time, the mantle would have been sufficiently molten for plates to move. However, the author of Text 2 asserts that empirical evidence from the geological record is necessary to make plausible claims about when tectonic movement began. Text 2 mentions an analysis performed by Wriju Chowdhury and his team of the geochemistry of zircon crystals (which would constitute empirical evidence). Chowdhury and his team argue, based on this analysis, that tectonic plates may have begun to move as early as 4.2 billion years ago. Therefore, since the author of Text 2 would consider Chowdhury et al.&rsquo;s empirical evidence to be more conclusive than the computer models cited in Text 1, the author of Text 2 would most likely assert that a more definitive form of evidence than the computer models suggests a different timeline for the onset of plate tectonics on Earth.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the author of Text 2 makes no claims about the temperature of Earth&rsquo;s mantle and therefore wouldn&rsquo;t argue that the temperature of Earth&rsquo;s mantle 3 billion years ago was insufficient to allow tectonic movement. Choice B is incorrect because the author of Text 2 claims that empirical evidence is needed to fix the earliest date of tectonic movement. Computer models are simulations, not empirical evidence, so the author of Text 2 wouldn&rsquo;t distinguish between different kinds of computer models but would instead argue that no computer models can reliably predict the onset of plate tectonics. Choice C is incorrect because the author of Text 2 wouldn&rsquo;t consider any computer model to be able to provide evidence to support a plausible claim about tectonic movement, no matter how much such models were improved. The author of Text 2 would only accept empirical evidence.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"c19b2f77","external_id":"75d0401c-75d9-4070-be56-daf441f2908a","vaultid":null,"uId":"c6efc492-3442-4396-b38e-6f40b36de20d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"CTC","skill_desc":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><span class=\"paired_passage\" role=\"heading\" aria-level=\"2\"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>\n<p>Little is known about how plate tectonics&mdash;wherein slabs of Earth&rsquo;s crust move over, under, away from, and against one another&mdash;began. <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\">Some researchers contend</span> that tectonic movements began around 3 billion years ago, often noting that computer models of Earth&rsquo;s mantle temperature at the time indicate that the mantle would have been sufficiently molten to enable the plates to move.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><span class=\"paired_passage\" role=\"heading\" aria-level=\"2\"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>\n<p>Ultimately, any plausible claim about the inception of tectonic movement must rest on empirical evidence from the geological record. Researcher Wriju Chowdhury and his team analyzed the geochemistry of zircon crystals to gain insight into the chemical composition of the magma from which the crystals formed and, based on the data, compellingly argue that plate tectonics may have been occurring as early as 4.2 billion years ago.</p>","stem":"<p>Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to what &ldquo;some researchers contend&rdquo; as described in Text 1?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"368bbf12-3876-486c-a831-89650d86f302","content":"<p>By suggesting that the temperature of Earth&rsquo;s mantle 3 billion years ago was likely insufficient to allow for the level of tectonic movement predicted by computer models</p>"},{"id":"c60e5c6e-9b40-4ea2-9eee-82be9753f271","content":"<p>By distinguishing between computer models of Earth&rsquo;s mantle temperature that reliably predict the onset of plate tectonics and those that do not</p>"},{"id":"a14e3807-1d3c-47bf-908c-86f496ba2777","content":"<p>By indicating that computer models of Earth&rsquo;s mantle temperature are still being improved such that new models tend to be much more reliable than their predecessors</p>"},{"id":"cf64fc36-831a-42eb-a70c-f25a58af6ae4","content":"<p>By asserting that a more definitive form of evidence than the computer models suggests a different timeline for the onset of plate tectonics on Earth</p>"}],"keys":["cf64fc36-831a-42eb-a70c-f25a58af6ae4"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it reflects how the author of Text 2 would most likely respond to what the researchers mentioned in Text 1 contend. Text 1 discusses the lack of knowledge of how plate tectonics on Earth began. Text 1 also mentions researchers who contend that movements of tectonic plates began around 3 billion years ago. As support for this assertion, these researchers cite computer models (which are simulations, not empirical evidence) of the temperature in Earth&rsquo;s mantle that show that at that time, the mantle would have been sufficiently molten for plates to move. However, the author of Text 2 asserts that empirical evidence from the geological record is necessary to make plausible claims about when tectonic movement began. Text 2 mentions an analysis performed by Wriju Chowdhury and his team of the geochemistry of zircon crystals (which would constitute empirical evidence). Chowdhury and his team argue, based on this analysis, that tectonic plates may have begun to move as early as 4.2 billion years ago. Therefore, since the author of Text 2 would consider Chowdhury et al.&rsquo;s empirical evidence to be more conclusive than the computer models cited in Text 1, the author of Text 2 would most likely assert that a more definitive form of evidence than the computer models suggests a different timeline for the onset of plate tectonics on Earth.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the author of Text 2 makes no claims about the temperature of Earth&rsquo;s mantle and therefore wouldn&rsquo;t argue that the temperature of Earth&rsquo;s mantle 3 billion years ago was insufficient to allow tectonic movement. Choice B is incorrect because the author of Text 2 claims that empirical evidence is needed to fix the earliest date of tectonic movement. Computer models are simulations, not empirical evidence, so the author of Text 2 wouldn&rsquo;t distinguish between different kinds of computer models but would instead argue that no computer models can reliably predict the onset of plate tectonics. Choice C is incorrect because the author of Text 2 wouldn&rsquo;t consider any computer model to be able to provide evidence to support a plausible claim about tectonic movement, no matter how much such models were improved. The author of Text 2 would only accept empirical evidence.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669208,"updateDate":1743430669208}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669208, 1743430669208),
    ($SATQ$c218cd98$SATQ$, $SATQ$e19e6680-2fd3-48eb-8f9d-5928fc54106c$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In 1776, the United States sent Benjamin Franklin to France to try to win the country&rsquo;s support in the United States&rsquo; fight for independence from Great Britain. Franklin was very popular in France. This <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> surely helped him to convince France to assist the United States.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0a0544b8-141b-4cf7-9540-188d9770cbed","content":"<p>thoughtfulness</p>"},{"id":"96115c68-b8e0-4c2f-b1d4-84bb31b71f2d","content":"<p>esteem</p>"},{"id":"94ba7270-1c2e-4df1-8cff-7fe8db784aac","content":"<p>controversy</p>"},{"id":"43fe11cf-1605-4741-8e41-49943cd828e4","content":"<p>sincerity</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["96115c68-b8e0-4c2f-b1d4-84bb31b71f2d"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Benjamin Franklin&rsquo;s popularity in France. In this context, "esteem" means high regard. The text indicates that Franklin was very popular, or highly regarded, in France, where he sought the country&rsquo;s support for the United States in its fight for independence, and indicates that his status helped him achieve his goal. The context therefore suggests that being held in high regard by the people likely helped Franklin convince France to help the United States.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text directly indicates that it was Franklin&rsquo;s popularity that likely helped him convince France to help the United States, not his "thoughtfulness" (which in this context would mean either his careful reasoning and attention or his kind consideration of others&rsquo; needs). Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that there was any "controversy," or dispute, about Franklin&rsquo;s presence in France; instead, the text states that Franklin was very popular in France and directly indicates that this status likely helped him convince France to help the United States. Choice D is incorrect because the text directly indicates that it was Franklin&rsquo;s popularity that likely helped him convince France to help the United States, not his "sincerity," or his honesty.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"c218cd98","external_id":"e19e6680-2fd3-48eb-8f9d-5928fc54106c","vaultid":null,"uId":"e1e3f61c-695e-4d2b-8ab1-5e1df04180f7","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In 1776, the United States sent Benjamin Franklin to France to try to win the country&rsquo;s support in the United States&rsquo; fight for independence from Great Britain. Franklin was very popular in France. This <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> surely helped him to convince France to assist the United States.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0a0544b8-141b-4cf7-9540-188d9770cbed","content":"<p>thoughtfulness</p>"},{"id":"96115c68-b8e0-4c2f-b1d4-84bb31b71f2d","content":"<p>esteem</p>"},{"id":"94ba7270-1c2e-4df1-8cff-7fe8db784aac","content":"<p>controversy</p>"},{"id":"43fe11cf-1605-4741-8e41-49943cd828e4","content":"<p>sincerity</p>"}],"keys":["96115c68-b8e0-4c2f-b1d4-84bb31b71f2d"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Benjamin Franklin&rsquo;s popularity in France. In this context, \"esteem\" means high regard. The text indicates that Franklin was very popular, or highly regarded, in France, where he sought the country&rsquo;s support for the United States in its fight for independence, and indicates that his status helped him achieve his goal. The context therefore suggests that being held in high regard by the people likely helped Franklin convince France to help the United States.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text directly indicates that it was Franklin&rsquo;s popularity that likely helped him convince France to help the United States, not his \"thoughtfulness\" (which in this context would mean either his careful reasoning and attention or his kind consideration of others&rsquo; needs). Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that there was any \"controversy,\" or dispute, about Franklin&rsquo;s presence in France; instead, the text states that Franklin was very popular in France and directly indicates that this status likely helped him convince France to help the United States. Choice D is incorrect because the text directly indicates that it was Franklin&rsquo;s popularity that likely helped him convince France to help the United States, not his \"sincerity,\" or his honesty.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669214,"updateDate":1743430669214}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669214, 1743430669214),
    ($SATQ$c2c26e20$SATQ$, $SATQ$412884c0-234a-4f98-9fd6-2a4030b982fc$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$36694562-ac27-4d3c-90b3-6ee1bccd024f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from Sadakichi Hartmann&rsquo;s 1894 short story &ldquo;Magnolia Blossoms.&rdquo; The narrator is standing on the deck of a boat.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>What a night it was! My soul had left its body to lose itself in the wild unrestrained beauty around me&mdash;from where it came&mdash;and only left a trembling <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>suggestion</u></span> of its existence within me. The other passengers moved around me like shadows, and again and again my eyes drank in all the glory and wealth of that night.</p>
</blockquote>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;suggestion&rdquo; most nearly mean?&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"7e4da43f-ae00-4a59-9a48-af4b1a40a1cf","content":"<p>Trace</p>"},{"id":"0fe465b0-3089-4586-99ae-e7412a1cac74","content":"<p>Opinion</p>"},{"id":"b215b9b6-ff97-46d7-ba4f-f3e407eeb77a","content":"<p>Dispute</p>"},{"id":"027bed12-1b43-4e2a-820a-9829059db1dc","content":"<p>Command</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["7e4da43f-ae00-4a59-9a48-af4b1a40a1cf"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because as used in the text, &ldquo;suggestion&rdquo; most nearly means trace. The text portrays the narrator standing on the deck of a boat, admiring the view of nature afforded by this position: &ldquo;My soul had left its body to lose itself in the wild unrestrained beauty around me,&rdquo; says the narrator, &ldquo;and only left a trembling suggestion of its existence within me.&rdquo; This intense response to beauty is such that the narrator&rsquo;s soul seems to disengage from its body, leaving behind only a barely detectible indication of its presence there. In other words, the narrator senses only a trace of soul left in the body. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. Although in some contexts &ldquo;suggestion&rdquo; can refer to an implied or indirectly expressed opinion, the text doesn&rsquo;t portray the narrator expressing an opinion; instead, the narrator is explaining an experience of intense emotion. Choice C is incorrect. While &ldquo;suggestion&rdquo; might be used in some contexts to refer to the tactful expression of a differing viewpoint, it doesn&rsquo;t refer to the dispute or difference of opinion itself. Moreover, the text doesn&rsquo;t portray a dispute between characters with differing viewpoints. Choice D is incorrect. Although in some contexts, &ldquo;suggestion&rdquo; might be used to refer to a politely worded command, the text doesn&rsquo;t portray a scenario in which someone receives such a command. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-036-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$c813d2a8-4237-4f56-acf7-9663a1bb09c3$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"c2c26e20","external_id":"412884c0-234a-4f98-9fd6-2a4030b982fc","vaultid":"36694562-ac27-4d3c-90b3-6ee1bccd024f","uId":"ba2a97f4-59d4-45a9-b928-1ffe62718464","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from Sadakichi Hartmann&rsquo;s 1894 short story &ldquo;Magnolia Blossoms.&rdquo; The narrator is standing on the deck of a boat.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What a night it was! My soul had left its body to lose itself in the wild unrestrained beauty around me&mdash;from where it came&mdash;and only left a trembling <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>suggestion</u></span> of its existence within me. The other passengers moved around me like shadows, and again and again my eyes drank in all the glory and wealth of that night.</p>\n</blockquote>","stem":"<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;suggestion&rdquo; most nearly mean?&nbsp;</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"7e4da43f-ae00-4a59-9a48-af4b1a40a1cf","content":"<p>Trace</p>"},{"id":"0fe465b0-3089-4586-99ae-e7412a1cac74","content":"<p>Opinion</p>"},{"id":"b215b9b6-ff97-46d7-ba4f-f3e407eeb77a","content":"<p>Dispute</p>"},{"id":"027bed12-1b43-4e2a-820a-9829059db1dc","content":"<p>Command</p>"}],"keys":["7e4da43f-ae00-4a59-9a48-af4b1a40a1cf"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because as used in the text, &ldquo;suggestion&rdquo; most nearly means trace. The text portrays the narrator standing on the deck of a boat, admiring the view of nature afforded by this position: &ldquo;My soul had left its body to lose itself in the wild unrestrained beauty around me,&rdquo; says the narrator, &ldquo;and only left a trembling suggestion of its existence within me.&rdquo; This intense response to beauty is such that the narrator&rsquo;s soul seems to disengage from its body, leaving behind only a barely detectible indication of its presence there. In other words, the narrator senses only a trace of soul left in the body. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. Although in some contexts &ldquo;suggestion&rdquo; can refer to an implied or indirectly expressed opinion, the text doesn&rsquo;t portray the narrator expressing an opinion; instead, the narrator is explaining an experience of intense emotion. Choice C is incorrect. While &ldquo;suggestion&rdquo; might be used in some contexts to refer to the tactful expression of a differing viewpoint, it doesn&rsquo;t refer to the dispute or difference of opinion itself. Moreover, the text doesn&rsquo;t portray a dispute between characters with differing viewpoints. Choice D is incorrect. Although in some contexts, &ldquo;suggestion&rdquo; might be used to refer to a politely worded command, the text doesn&rsquo;t portray a scenario in which someone receives such a command. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-036-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"c813d2a8-4237-4f56-acf7-9663a1bb09c3","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959320,"updateDate":1691007959320}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959320, 1691007959320),
    ($SATQ$c4737d6a$SATQ$, $SATQ$89ad53b5-5784-473a-bce5-aa2c145ed03a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$3f200bcf-df91-4223-b92d-883fc737ab8f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Cross-Text Connections$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><strong>Text 1</strong></p>
<p>Africa&rsquo;s Sahara region&mdash;once a lush ecosystem&mdash;began to dry out about 8,000 years ago. A change in Earth&rsquo;s orbit that affected climate has been posited as a cause of desertification, but archaeologist David Wright also attributes the shift to Neolithic peoples. He cites their adoption of pastoralism as a factor in the region drying out: the pastoralists&rsquo; livestock depleted vegetation, prompting the events that created the Sahara Desert.</p>
<p><strong>Text 2</strong></p>
<p>Research by Chris Brierley et al. challenges the idea that Neolithic peoples contributed to the Sahara&rsquo;s desertification. Using a climate-vegetation model, the team concluded that the end of the region&rsquo;s humid period occurred 500 years earlier than previously assumed. The timing suggests that Neolithic peoples didn&rsquo;t exacerbate aridity in the region but, in fact, may have helped delay environmental changes with practices (e.g., selective grazing) that preserved vegetation.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the texts, how would Chris Brierley (Text 2) most likely respond to the discussion in Text 1?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"cda873d2-dc5e-4b80-bc6a-aeaa043db553","content":"<p>By pointing out that given the revised timeline for the end of the Sahara&rsquo;s humid period, the Neolithic peoples&rsquo; mode of subsistence likely didn&rsquo;t cause the region&rsquo;s desertification</p>"},{"id":"f2d4a1b2-d08b-4ee0-b4b8-10508b796293","content":"<p>By claiming that pastoralism was only one of many behaviors the Neolithic peoples took part in that may have contributed to the Sahara&rsquo;s changing climate</p>"},{"id":"3e15539a-4635-475d-af53-bd4e701c4bc5","content":"<p>By insisting that pastoralism can have both beneficial and deleterious effects on a region&rsquo;s vegetation and climate</p>"},{"id":"5fff5a97-43b0-48a7-a70a-4e6ab707d066","content":"<p>By asserting that more research needs to be conducted into factors that likely contributed to the desertification of the Sahara region</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["cda873d2-dc5e-4b80-bc6a-aeaa043db553"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. Brierley et al.&rsquo;s research directly challenges Wright&rsquo;s claim that pastoralism turned the Sahara into a desert, suggesting that, in a Sahara that turned arid 500 years earlier than previously thought, pastoral practices may have actually &ldquo;preserved vegetation&rdquo; rather than depleting it. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. Brierley et al.&rsquo;s research actually disputes the idea that any Neolithic peoples&rsquo; behaviors, including pastoralism, could have contributed to the Sahara&rsquo;s changing climate. In fact, their research implies that the Neolithic peoples&rsquo; practices did not &ldquo;exacerbate aridity&rdquo; (i.e., make things worse), but may have slowed environmental changes. Choice C is incorrect. Brierley et al.&rsquo;s research does not acknowledge that pastoralism can have deleterious (i.e., negative) effects on a region&rsquo;s vegetation and climate. It only describes one possible beneficial effect: preserving vegetation through practices like selective grazing. Choice D is incorrect. Brierley et al.&rsquo;s research does not call for more research into factors that likely contributed to the desertification of the Sahara region. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-CTC-H008$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$3fb6606c-cab8-4705-8644-3640d0b517e8$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"c4737d6a","external_id":"89ad53b5-5784-473a-bce5-aa2c145ed03a","vaultid":"3f200bcf-df91-4223-b92d-883fc737ab8f","uId":"1fedb3b8-1dce-4700-9919-dc2666a358e4","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"CTC","skill_desc":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><strong>Text 1</strong></p>\n<p>Africa&rsquo;s Sahara region&mdash;once a lush ecosystem&mdash;began to dry out about 8,000 years ago. A change in Earth&rsquo;s orbit that affected climate has been posited as a cause of desertification, but archaeologist David Wright also attributes the shift to Neolithic peoples. He cites their adoption of pastoralism as a factor in the region drying out: the pastoralists&rsquo; livestock depleted vegetation, prompting the events that created the Sahara Desert.</p>\n<p><strong>Text 2</strong></p>\n<p>Research by Chris Brierley et al. challenges the idea that Neolithic peoples contributed to the Sahara&rsquo;s desertification. Using a climate-vegetation model, the team concluded that the end of the region&rsquo;s humid period occurred 500 years earlier than previously assumed. The timing suggests that Neolithic peoples didn&rsquo;t exacerbate aridity in the region but, in fact, may have helped delay environmental changes with practices (e.g., selective grazing) that preserved vegetation.</p>","stem":"<p>Based on the texts, how would Chris Brierley (Text 2) most likely respond to the discussion in Text 1?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"cda873d2-dc5e-4b80-bc6a-aeaa043db553","content":"<p>By pointing out that given the revised timeline for the end of the Sahara&rsquo;s humid period, the Neolithic peoples&rsquo; mode of subsistence likely didn&rsquo;t cause the region&rsquo;s desertification</p>"},{"id":"f2d4a1b2-d08b-4ee0-b4b8-10508b796293","content":"<p>By claiming that pastoralism was only one of many behaviors the Neolithic peoples took part in that may have contributed to the Sahara&rsquo;s changing climate</p>"},{"id":"3e15539a-4635-475d-af53-bd4e701c4bc5","content":"<p>By insisting that pastoralism can have both beneficial and deleterious effects on a region&rsquo;s vegetation and climate</p>"},{"id":"5fff5a97-43b0-48a7-a70a-4e6ab707d066","content":"<p>By asserting that more research needs to be conducted into factors that likely contributed to the desertification of the Sahara region</p>"}],"keys":["cda873d2-dc5e-4b80-bc6a-aeaa043db553"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. Brierley et al.&rsquo;s research directly challenges Wright&rsquo;s claim that pastoralism turned the Sahara into a desert, suggesting that, in a Sahara that turned arid 500 years earlier than previously thought, pastoral practices may have actually &ldquo;preserved vegetation&rdquo; rather than depleting it. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. Brierley et al.&rsquo;s research actually disputes the idea that any Neolithic peoples&rsquo; behaviors, including pastoralism, could have contributed to the Sahara&rsquo;s changing climate. In fact, their research implies that the Neolithic peoples&rsquo; practices did not &ldquo;exacerbate aridity&rdquo; (i.e., make things worse), but may have slowed environmental changes. Choice C is incorrect. Brierley et al.&rsquo;s research does not acknowledge that pastoralism can have deleterious (i.e., negative) effects on a region&rsquo;s vegetation and climate. It only describes one possible beneficial effect: preserving vegetation through practices like selective grazing. Choice D is incorrect. Brierley et al.&rsquo;s research does not call for more research into factors that likely contributed to the desertification of the Sahara region. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-CTC-H008","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"3fb6606c-cab8-4705-8644-3640d0b517e8","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959365,"updateDate":1691007959365}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959365, 1691007959365),
    ($SATQ$c4900368$SATQ$, $SATQ$974b9c60-47e6-4642-bb02-bdbca4f56fd4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$0dec599a-7c57-4697-b4d5-3e32081c50db$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is from the 1924 poem &ldquo;Cycle&rdquo; by D&rsquo;Arcy McNickle, who was a citizen of the Confederated Salish and Kootenai Tribes.</p>
<p style="padding-left:1em;">There shall be new roads wending,<br>A new beating of the drum&mdash;<br>Men&rsquo;s eyes shall have fresh seeing,<br>Grey lives reprise their span&mdash;<br>But under the new sun&rsquo;s being,<br>Completing what night began,<br>There&rsquo;ll be the same backs bending,<br>The same sad feet shall drum&mdash;<br>When this night finds its ending<br>And day shall have come.....</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"5a21637c-6103-4608-9d31-9fcb6f153dbb","content":"<p>To consider how the repetitiveness inherent in human life can be both rewarding and challenging</p>"},{"id":"7b47f7fe-76a4-4983-8e11-9c00e9b8fabf","content":"<p>To question whether activities completed at one time of day are more memorable than those completed at another time of day</p>"},{"id":"08590421-958c-4900-8ad9-97726db0a87e","content":"<p>To refute the idea that joy is a more commonly experienced emotion than sadness is</p>"},{"id":"45fc6cab-00ef-45ff-ae0e-a353eaa4aeaa","content":"<p>To demonstrate how the experiences of individuals relate to the experiences of their communities</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["5a21637c-6103-4608-9d31-9fcb6f153dbb"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it accurately states the main purpose of the text. The text begins by discussing the promise of the future, with positive references to renewal such as &ldquo;new roads,&rdquo; &ldquo;new beating of the drum,&rdquo; and &ldquo;fresh seeing.&rdquo; But with the &ldquo;new sun,&rdquo; the text continues, there will still be &ldquo;the same backs bending&rdquo; and &ldquo;the same sad feet&rdquo; drumming, indicating that these difficulties will follow people into this new day. The poem thus considers both the rewards and challenges associated with the repetitiveness of human life. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t say anything about how memorable activities are, let alone compare the memorability of activities completed at different times of the day. Choice C is incorrect. Although the text contrasts hope with difficulty, it does not compare the relative frequency of joyful feelings with that of sad feelings.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because the text makes no distinction between the experiences of individuals and the experiences of their communities. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-028-TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$8304f511-2f5c-4bed-9f97-859c69acb020$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"c4900368","external_id":"974b9c60-47e6-4642-bb02-bdbca4f56fd4","vaultid":"0dec599a-7c57-4697-b4d5-3e32081c50db","uId":"76b5da5f-b488-4d02-bf4d-1ee40073babb","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is from the 1924 poem &ldquo;Cycle&rdquo; by D&rsquo;Arcy McNickle, who was a citizen of the Confederated Salish and Kootenai Tribes.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-left:1em;\">There shall be new roads wending,<br>A new beating of the drum&mdash;<br>Men&rsquo;s eyes shall have fresh seeing,<br>Grey lives reprise their span&mdash;<br>But under the new sun&rsquo;s being,<br>Completing what night began,<br>There&rsquo;ll be the same backs bending,<br>The same sad feet shall drum&mdash;<br>When this night finds its ending<br>And day shall have come.....</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"5a21637c-6103-4608-9d31-9fcb6f153dbb","content":"<p>To consider how the repetitiveness inherent in human life can be both rewarding and challenging</p>"},{"id":"7b47f7fe-76a4-4983-8e11-9c00e9b8fabf","content":"<p>To question whether activities completed at one time of day are more memorable than those completed at another time of day</p>"},{"id":"08590421-958c-4900-8ad9-97726db0a87e","content":"<p>To refute the idea that joy is a more commonly experienced emotion than sadness is</p>"},{"id":"45fc6cab-00ef-45ff-ae0e-a353eaa4aeaa","content":"<p>To demonstrate how the experiences of individuals relate to the experiences of their communities</p>"}],"keys":["5a21637c-6103-4608-9d31-9fcb6f153dbb"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it accurately states the main purpose of the text. The text begins by discussing the promise of the future, with positive references to renewal such as &ldquo;new roads,&rdquo; &ldquo;new beating of the drum,&rdquo; and &ldquo;fresh seeing.&rdquo; But with the &ldquo;new sun,&rdquo; the text continues, there will still be &ldquo;the same backs bending&rdquo; and &ldquo;the same sad feet&rdquo; drumming, indicating that these difficulties will follow people into this new day. The poem thus considers both the rewards and challenges associated with the repetitiveness of human life. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t say anything about how memorable activities are, let alone compare the memorability of activities completed at different times of the day. Choice C is incorrect. Although the text contrasts hope with difficulty, it does not compare the relative frequency of joyful feelings with that of sad feelings.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because the text makes no distinction between the experiences of individuals and the experiences of their communities. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-028-TSP","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"8304f511-2f5c-4bed-9f97-859c69acb020","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959318,"updateDate":1691007959318}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959318, 1691007959318),
    ($SATQ$c502943e$SATQ$, $SATQ$3ec391b1-5631-41ab-8f96-831f600e0aa8$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from Cynthia Kadohata&rsquo;s 2004 novel <em>Kira-Kira</em>.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;" role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content">[Uncle Katsuhisa] was as loud as my father was quiet.</span> Even when he wasn&rsquo;t talking, he made a lot of noise, clearing his throat and sniffing and tapping his fingers.</p>
</blockquote>
<p style="text-align: right;"><span class="dap_copyright">&copy;2004 by Cynthia Kadohata</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"1e2fb520-6f31-45e7-a1f2-d0e25b3e5c2d","content":"<p>It lists the kinds of topics Uncle Katsuhisa enjoys discussing.</p>"},{"id":"6322269b-82c2-4f44-99ec-34bd4228ad78","content":"<p>It suggests that Uncle Katsuhisa dislikes meeting new people.</p>"},{"id":"8e2163c3-8f41-4fed-b998-7a2600344e45","content":"<p>It contrasts Uncle Katsuhisa with the narrator&rsquo;s father.</p>"},{"id":"80ae878f-6a2a-444b-9a4d-51ba7bbdac91","content":"<p>It describes a conversation between the narrator and the narrator&rsquo;s father.&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["8e2163c3-8f41-4fed-b998-7a2600344e45"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most accurately describes how the underlined sentence functions in the text as a whole. The underlined sentence establishes a difference between Uncle Katsuhisa and the narrator&rsquo;s father by describing Uncle Katsuhisa as "loud" and the narrator&rsquo;s father as "quiet." The text then elaborates on that contrast, describing some ways Uncle Katsuhisa is very noisy even when he isn&rsquo;t speaking.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t indicate what kinds of topics Uncle Katsuhisa enjoys discussing, only that he is loud even when he isn&rsquo;t speaking. Choice B is incorrect because the text never indicates how Uncle Katsuhisa feels about meeting new people, only how loud he is. Choice D is incorrect because the text never describes a conversation occurring between any people; it refers to talking only when stating that Uncle Katsuhisa is loud even when he isn&rsquo;t speaking.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"c502943e","external_id":"3ec391b1-5631-41ab-8f96-831f600e0aa8","vaultid":null,"uId":"3f75cf38-c11b-4bb3-9283-c7bfb82bca68","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from Cynthia Kadohata&rsquo;s 2004 novel <em>Kira-Kira</em>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\">[Uncle Katsuhisa] was as loud as my father was quiet.</span> Even when he wasn&rsquo;t talking, he made a lot of noise, clearing his throat and sniffing and tapping his fingers.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p style=\"text-align: right;\"><span class=\"dap_copyright\">&copy;2004 by Cynthia Kadohata</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"1e2fb520-6f31-45e7-a1f2-d0e25b3e5c2d","content":"<p>It lists the kinds of topics Uncle Katsuhisa enjoys discussing.</p>"},{"id":"6322269b-82c2-4f44-99ec-34bd4228ad78","content":"<p>It suggests that Uncle Katsuhisa dislikes meeting new people.</p>"},{"id":"8e2163c3-8f41-4fed-b998-7a2600344e45","content":"<p>It contrasts Uncle Katsuhisa with the narrator&rsquo;s father.</p>"},{"id":"80ae878f-6a2a-444b-9a4d-51ba7bbdac91","content":"<p>It describes a conversation between the narrator and the narrator&rsquo;s father.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["8e2163c3-8f41-4fed-b998-7a2600344e45"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most accurately describes how the underlined sentence functions in the text as a whole. The underlined sentence establishes a difference between Uncle Katsuhisa and the narrator&rsquo;s father by describing Uncle Katsuhisa as \"loud\" and the narrator&rsquo;s father as \"quiet.\" The text then elaborates on that contrast, describing some ways Uncle Katsuhisa is very noisy even when he isn&rsquo;t speaking.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the t